uld suggest how an element is used, allowing users to understand actions without instructions (e.g., buttons that look clickable).','Which design principle suggests that the design should make it obvious how an element is used without requiring instructions?',0,'PENDING','2026-11-13 09:45:01.944963',NULL,29,NULL,1,'MULTIPLE_CHOICE',NULL),(183,'WIMP Model','IPOF Model','UCD Model','CLI Model',1,'2026-11-13 09:45:01.952937','HARD','Includes: (IPOF Model) • Input • Process • Output • Feedback',_binary '','The Interaction Component includes the IPOF Model, which stands for Input, Process, Output, and Feedback.','Which of the following models is included as part of the Interaction Component representing communication between users and systems?',0,'PENDING','2026-11-13 09:45:01.952937',NULL,29,NULL,1,'MULTIPLE_CHOICE',NULL),(184,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,'2026-11-13 10:07:31.545411','MEDIUM','Early Computing Era (1940s–1960s) ... Command Line Interfaces (CLI) Era (1960s–1980s) ... Personal Computing Revolution (late 1970s–1980s) ... WIMP Model / Graphical User Interfaces (1980s–1990s) ... Modern Natural User Interfaces (2010s–present)',_binary '\0','Students should trace how computing moved from room-sized mainframes used by specialists via punch cards to expert-centered CLI, then through the personal computing and WIMP revolution to mobile touch systems and modern natural user interfaces/AI.','Discuss the evolution of Human-Computer Interaction (HCI) from the early computing era of the 1940s-1960s to modern Natural User Interfaces and emerging technologies. How did shifts in user demographics and hardware capabilities change the way humans interact with computers over time?',0,'PENDING','2026-11-13 10:07:31.545411',NULL,31,NULL,10,'ESSAY','[{\"id\":null,\"name\":\"Historical Progression\",\"description\":\"Accurately describes key eras of HCI history, including early computing, CLI, WIMP, mobile computing, and modern/emerging technologies.\",\"maxPoints\":4,\"displayOrder\":0},{\"id\":null,\"name\":\"Demographic and Hardware Shifts\",\"description\":\"Explains how the transition from trained specialists to non-expert everyday users and advances in hardware drove the demand for more intuitive interfaces.\",\"maxPoints\":3,\"displayOrder\":1},{\"id\":null,\"name\":\"Analysis of Interaction Style\",\"description\":\"Analyzes how interaction methods evolved from batch processing and text-based commands to graphical, touch, and natural user interfaces.\",\"maxPoints\":3,\"displayOrder\":2}]'),(185,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,'2026-11-13 10:07:31.558368','HARD','HCI consists of three interconnected components: The Human (User), The Computer (System), The Interaction. All influenced by the context of use.',_binary '','A strong response details how human physical/cognitive traits meet hardware/software systems via continuous, two-way interaction defined by the IPOF model (Input, Process, Output, Feedback).','Analyze the three core components of Human-Computer Interaction—the Human, the Computer, and the Interaction—and explain how they work together within the context of use. In your answer, incorporate the IPOF model and human capabilities/limitations.',0,'PENDING','2026-11-13 10:07:31.558368',NULL,31,NULL,10,'ESSAY','[{\"id\":null,\"name\":\"The Human Component\",\"description\":\"Identifies physical, cognitive, emotional, and social characteristics of users that influence system interaction.\",\"maxPoints\":3,\"displayOrder\":0},{\"id\":null,\"name\":\"The Computer Component\",\"description\":\"Describes the hardware and software elements that process information and support user input and output.\",\"maxPoints\":3,\"displayOrder\":1},{\"id\":null,\"name\":\"The Interaction Component & IPOF Model\",\"description\":\"Explains interaction as a continuous, two-way communication using the Input, Process, Output, and Feedback (IPOF) model.\",\"maxPoints\":4,\"displayOrder\":2}]'),(186,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,'2026-11-13 10:07:31.565344','EASY','Principles of Design in Human-Computer Interaction: User Control, Consistency, Visibility, Simplicity, Affordance, Feedback, Error Prevention and Recovery',_binary '\0','Students should explain the specific design principles, give examples for each (e.g., confirmation dialogs for error prevention, consistent icons for consistency), and connect them to overall usability goals.','Examine the role of design principles such as User Control, Consistency, Visibility, and Feedback in creating effective interactive systems. Provide practical examples of how these principles prevent errors and improve user experience.',0,'PENDING','2026-11-13 10:07:31.565344',NULL,31,NULL,10,'ESSAY','[{\"id\":null,\"name\":\"Identification of Design Principles\",\"description\":\"Accurately defines and explains key design principles like User Control, Consistency, Visibility, Simplicity, Affordance, Feedback, and Error Prevention.\",\"maxPoints\":4,\"displayOrder\":0},{\"id\":null,\"name\":\"Application and Examples\",\"description\":\"Provides clear, relevant examples (such as back buttons, save icons, loading indicators, or undo functionality) for the discussed principles.\",\"maxPoints\":3,\"displayOrder\":1},{\"id\":null,\"name\":\"Impact on User Experience\",\"description\":\"Explains how applying these principles reduces user errors, improves learnability/efficiency, and ensures a positive user experience.\",\"maxPoints\":3,\"displayOrder\":2}]'),(187,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,'2026-11-13 10:07:33.614370','EASY','Creates confusion or frustration',_binary '','The student should mention traits like inconsistent layouts, hidden functions, poor feedback, overloaded screens, and difficult error recovery, and link them to user confusion, frustration, and frequent errors.','Discuss the primary characteristics and impacts of ineffective design in human-computer interaction based on the course orientation text. How do these traits affect the user experience?',1,'PENDING','2026-11-13 10:07:33.614370',NULL,31,NULL,10,'ESSAY','[{\"id\":null,\"name\":\"Identification of Characteristics\",\"description\":\"Accurately identifies and explains characteristics of ineffective design such as inconsistent layouts, hidden functions, poor feedback, overloaded screens, and difficult error recovery.\",\"maxPoints\":5,\"displayOrder\":0},{\"id\":null,\"name\":\"Impact on Users\",\"description\":\"Explains how ineffective design creates confusion, frustration, excessive learning, frequent errors, and lacks guidance.\",\"maxPoints\":5,\"displayOrder\":1}]'),(188,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,'2026-11-13 10:07:33.625333','MEDIUM','Logical navigation',_binary '','The student should highlight logical navigation and error-tolerant design as positive qualities while contrasting them against ineffective design traits like difficult error recovery and frequent user errors.','Contrast effective design concepts with ineffective design traits by explaining what makes a design error-tolerant and logically navigable compared to one that causes frequent user errors.',1,'PENDING','2026-11-13 10:07:33.625333',NULL,31,NULL,10,'ESSAY','[{\"id\":null,\"name\":\"Effective Design Concepts\",\"description\":\"Explains the concepts of logical navigation and error-tolerant design as mentioned in the text.\",\"maxPoints\":5,\"displayOrder\":0},{\"id\":null,\"name\":\"Contrast with Ineffective Design\",\"description\":\"Contrasts effective elements with ineffective traits such as lack of guidance, difficult error recovery, and frequent user errors.\",\"maxPoints\":5,\"displayOrder\":1}]'),(189,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,'2026-11-13 10:59:09.067239','EASY','Human–Computer Interaction is “the study of people and computer technology and the ways these influence each other.” (Preece, Rogers, & Sharp, 2015)',_binary '','Preece, Rogers, and Sharp (2015) are cited for the formal definition of Human-Computer Interaction.','Who provided the formal definition stating that HCI is the study of people and computer technology and the ways these influence each other?',0,'PENDING','2026-11-13 10:59:09.067239',NULL,33,'[\"Preece, Rogers, & Sharp\",\"Preece, Rogers and Sharp\",\"Preece, Rogers, & Sharp, 2015\"]',1,'ENUMERATION',NULL),(190,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,'2026-11-13 10:59:09.078201','EASY','Early Computing Era (1940s–1960s)',_binary '','The Early Computing Era spanned from the 1940s to the 1960s and involved mainframe computers occupying entire rooms with punch card interaction.','During which era of HCI history did computers occupy entire rooms and use punch cards for interaction?',0,'PENDING','2026-11-13 10:59:09.078201',NULL,33,'[\"Early Computing Era\",\"Early Computing Era (1940s–1960s)\",\"1940s-1960s\"]',1,'ENUMERATION',NULL),(191,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,'2026-11-13 10:59:09.086175','EASY','WIMP Model / Graphical User Interfaces (1980s–1990s)',_binary '','The WIMP model stands for Windows, Icons, Menus, and Pointers, serving as the standard desktop interaction model in the 1980s-1990s.','What interaction model from the 1980s–1990s featured Windows, Icons, Menus, and Pointers?',0,'PENDING','2026-11-13 10:59:09.086175',NULL,33,'[\"WIMP Model\",\"WIMP Model / Graphical User Interfaces\",\"Graphical User Interfaces\"]',1,'ENUMERATION',NULL),(192,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,'2026-11-13 10:59:09.094148','EASY','Includes: (IPOF Model)',_binary '','The Interaction Component includes the IPOF Model which consists of Input, Process, Output, and Feedback.','What model does the Interaction Component include, standing for Input, Process, Output, and Feedback?',0,'PENDING','2026-11-13 10:59:09.094148',NULL,33,'[\"IPOF Model\",\"IPOF\"]',1,'ENUMERATION',NULL),(193,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,'2026-11-13 10:59:09.102121','MEDIUM','Cognitive processes • Memory • Attention • Problem-solving',_binary '','Cognitive processes under human capabilities include memory, attention, and problem-solving.','Name one of the cognitive processes included under human capabilities and limitations.',0,'PENDING','2026-11-13 10:59:09.102121',NULL,33,'[\"Memory\",\"Attention\",\"Problem-solving\"]',1,'ENUMERATION',NULL),(194,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,'2026-11-13 10:59:09.110095','MEDIUM','Usability User-Centered Design Universal Design Accessibility',_binary '','The four main principles of HCI outlined are Usability, User-Centered Design, Universal Design, and Accessibility.','What are the four core principles of Human-Computer Interaction listed in Part III?',0,'PENDING','2026-11-13 10:59:09.110095',NULL,33,'[\"Usability, User-Centered Design, Universal Design, Accessibility\",\"Usability, User-Centered Design, Universal Design, and Accessibility\"]',1,'ENUMERATION',NULL),(195,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,'2026-11-13 10:59:09.119065','MEDIUM','o Learnability',_binary '','Learnability is a key attribute of usability representing how easily users can learn to use a system.','What key attribute of usability deals with how easy it is for users to accomplish basic tasks the first time they encounter the design?',0,'PENDING','2026-11-13 10:59:09.119065',NULL,33,'[\"Learnability\",\"learnability\"]',1,'ENUMERATION',NULL),(196,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,'2026-11-13 10:59:09.126041','EASY','User-Centered Design (UCD)',_binary '','User-Centered Design (UCD) places focus on users throughout the entire development process.','What design approach focuses on users throughout development and involves an iterative design process with continuous user involvement?',0,'PENDING','2026-11-13 10:59:09.126041',NULL,33,'[\"User-Centered Design\",\"UCD\",\"User-Centered Design (UCD)\"]',1,'ENUMERATION',NULL),(197,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,'2026-11-13 10:59:09.134015','EASY','Consistency o Similar actions produce similar results',_binary '','Consistency ensures that similar actions produce similar results and reduces learning effort.','What design principle ensures that similar actions produce similar results and interface elements behave the same way?',0,'PENDING','2026-11-13 10:59:09.134015',NULL,33,'[\"Consistency\",\"consistency\"]',1,'ENUMERATION',NULL),(198,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,'2026-11-13 10:59:09.141988','MEDIUM','Affordance o Design should suggest how an element is used',_binary '','Affordance means the design itself suggests how an element should be used (e.g., buttons that look clickable).','What design principle suggests how an element is used so that users understand actions without instructions?',0,'PENDING','2026-11-13 10:59:09.141988',NULL,33,'[\"Affordance\",\"affordance\"]',1,'ENUMERATION',NULL),(199,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,'2026-11-13 10:59:09.149961','MEDIUM','HCI consists of three interconnected components: The Human (User) The Computer (System) The Interaction',_binary '','HCI consists of the Human (User), the Computer (System), and the Interaction, all influenced by the context of use.','What three interconnected components make up Human-Computer Interaction according to Part II?',0,'PENDING','2026-11-13 10:59:09.149961',NULL,33,'[\"The Human, The Computer, The Interaction\",\"Human, Computer, Interaction\"]',1,'ENUMERATION',NULL),(200,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,'2026-11-13 10:59:09.158932','MEDIUM','Visibility o Important functions should be easily visible',_binary '','Visibility ensures important functions are easily visible so users do not have to remember information unnecessarily.','Which design principle supports recognition rather than recall by ensuring important functions are easily visible?',0,'PENDING','2026-11-13 10:59:09.158932',NULL,33,'[\"Visibility\",\"visibility\"]',1,'ENUMERATION',NULL),(201,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,'2026-11-13 10:59:09.165908','EASY','Simplicity o Eliminate unnecessary elements',_binary '','Simplicity involves eliminating unnecessary elements and avoiding overwhelming the user.','What principle of design focuses on eliminating unnecessary elements and presenting information clearly and concisely?',0,'PENDING','2026-11-13 10:59:09.165908',NULL,33,'[\"Simplicity\",\"simplicity\"]',1,'ENUMERATION',NULL),(202,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,'2026-11-13 10:59:09.173881','MEDIUM','Feedback should be: • Immediate • Clear • Meaningful',_binary '','The system should always inform users about what is happening through feedback that is immediate, clear, and meaningful.','What should system feedback always be according to the design principles?',0,'PENDING','2026-11-13 10:59:09.173881',NULL,33,'[\"Immediate, Clear, Meaningful\",\"Immediate, clear, and meaningful\"]',1,'ENUMERATION',NULL),(203,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,'2026-11-13 10:59:44.932823','EASY','o Logical navigation',_binary '','The text lists logical navigation as a characteristic point.','What navigation style is associated with effective design according to the text?',1,'PENDING','2026-11-13 10:59:44.932823',NULL,33,'[\"Logical navigation\"]',1,'ENUMERATION',NULL),(204,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,'2026-11-13 10:59:44.943786','EASY','o Error-tolerant design',_binary '','The text mentions error-tolerant design as a key trait.','What type of design is described as error-tolerant?',1,'PENDING','2026-11-13 10:59:44.943786',NULL,33,'[\"Error-tolerant design\"]',1,'ENUMERATION',NULL),(205,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,'2026-11-13 10:59:44.951313','EASY','CSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION',_binary '','This header appears repeatedly throughout the course material text.','What is the course code and title mentioned in the text?',1,'PENDING','2026-11-13 10:59:44.951313',NULL,33,'[\"CSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\"]',1,'ENUMERATION',NULL),(206,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,'2026-11-13 10:59:44.958289','EASY','o Creates confusion or frustration',_binary '','Ineffective design creates confusion or frustration.','What does an ineffective design create for the user?',1,'PENDING','2026-11-13 10:59:44.958289',NULL,33,'[\"Confusion or frustration\",\"Confusion\"]',1,'ENUMERATION',NULL),(207,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,'2026-11-13 10:59:44.966263','MEDIUM','o Requires excessive learning or memorization',_binary '','Ineffective designs require excessive learning or memorization.','What does an ineffective design require in terms of learning or memorization?',1,'PENDING','2026-11-13 10:59:44.966263',NULL,33,'[\"Excessive learning or memorization\"]',1,'ENUMERATION',NULL),(208,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,'2026-11-13 10:59:44.974236','EASY','o Causes frequent user errors',_binary '','Ineffective design causes frequent user errors.','What frequency of user errors does an ineffective design cause?',1,'PENDING','2026-11-13 10:59:44.974236',NULL,33,'[\"Frequent user errors\"]',1,'ENUMERATION',NULL),(209,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,'2026-11-13 10:59:44.981213','MEDIUM','o Lacks feedback or guidance',_binary '','Ineffective design lacks feedback or guidance.','What two elements does an ineffective design lack?',1,'PENDING','2026-11-13 10:59:44.981213',NULL,33,'[\"Feedback or guidance\"]',1,'ENUMERATION',NULL),(210,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,'2026-11-13 10:59:44.989186','MEDIUM','o Inconsistent layouts and controls',_binary '','Inconsistent layouts and controls are characteristics of ineffective design.','What kind of layouts and controls are listed as characteristics of ineffective design?',1,'PENDING','2026-11-13 10:59:44.989186',NULL,33,'[\"Inconsistent layouts and controls\",\"Inconsistent\"]',1,'ENUMERATION',NULL),(211,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,'2026-11-13 10:59:44.995166','MEDIUM','o Hidden or unclear functions',_binary '','Functions can be hidden or unclear in ineffective designs.','How are functions described in an ineffective design?',1,'PENDING','2026-11-13 10:59:44.995166',NULL,33,'[\"Hidden or unclear functions\",\"Hidden or unclear\"]',1,'ENUMERATION',NULL),(212,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,'2026-11-13 10:59:45.002142','MEDIUM','o Poor feedback or no feedback',_binary '','Poor feedback or no feedback is a characteristic of ineffective design.','What feedback issue characterizes ineffective design?',1,'PENDING','2026-11-13 10:59:45.002142',NULL,33,'[\"Poor feedback or no feedback\",\"Poor or no feedback\"]',1,'ENUMERATION',NULL),(213,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,'2026-11-13 10:59:45.010116','EASY','o Overloaded screens',_binary '','Overloaded screens are a characteristic of ineffective design.','What screen condition is considered a characteristic of ineffective design?',1,'PENDING','2026-11-13 10:59:45.010116',NULL,33,'[\"Overloaded screens\",\"Overloaded\"]',1,'ENUMERATION',NULL),(214,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,'2026-11-13 10:59:45.019086','EASY','o Difficult error recovery',_binary '','Difficult error recovery is a characteristic of ineffective design.','How is error recovery described in ineffective design?',1,'PENDING','2026-11-13 10:59:45.019086',NULL,33,'[\"Difficult error recovery\",\"Difficult\"]',1,'ENUMERATION',NULL),(215,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,'2026-11-13 10:59:45.027059','EASY','Effective \nvs \nIneffective \nDesign',_binary '','The text compares Effective vs Ineffective Design.','Which two design types are compared in the lesson headers?',1,'PENDING','2026-11-13 10:59:45.027059',NULL,33,'[\"Effective vs Ineffective Design\",\"Effective and Ineffective Design\"]',1,'ENUMERATION',NULL),(216,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,'2026-11-13 10:59:45.034035','EASY','END OF LESSON',_binary '','The text concludes with END OF LESSON.','What is indicated at the very end of the provided lesson text?',1,'PENDING','2026-11-13 10:59:45.034035',NULL,33,'[\"END OF LESSON\"]',1,'ENUMERATION',NULL),(217,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,'2026-11-13 10:59:45.042009','MEDIUM','CSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION',_binary '','The course orientation is for Human-Computer Interaction under code CSIT238.','What course orientation is the context of these notes?',1,'PENDING','2026-11-13 10:59:45.042009',NULL,33,'[\"HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\",\"Human-Computer Interaction\"]',1,'ENUMERATION',NULL);
