INSERT INTO `questions` VALUES (1,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.154583','EASY','In C, %d or %i outputs a signed decimal integer. %f is for floating-point values, %c is for single characters, and %s is for null-terminated character strings. Misconception: Novices often mistakenly think %d stands for double.',1,'What is the correct format specifier to print a standard signed int variable in C using printf?','MCQ',NULL,1,1,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(2,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.159583','EASY','In C, dividing two integers truncates any fractional portion toward zero. 7 / 2 produces 3, not 3.5. Misconception: Beginners assume the fractional part is preserved or rounded up.',1,'What is the value of \'result\' after executing this C code?\n\nint a = 7;\nint b = 2;\nint result = a / b;','MCQ',NULL,1,2,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(3,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.162583','MEDIUM','Character literals in C are represented by integer ASCII values (\'A\' is 65). Adding 2 yields 67. Because the %d specifier is used, it prints the integer 67 instead of character \'C\'. Misconception: Expecting the letter \'C\' due to ignoring the %d integer specifier.',1,'What is printed by the following C program?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    char ch = \'A\';\n    printf(\"%d\", ch + 2);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',NULL,1,3,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(4,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.165583','MEDIUM','By definition in the ISO C standard, sizeof(char) is always guaranteed to be exactly 1 byte. Misconception: Students familiar with Java assume char is 2 bytes for 16-bit Unicode.',1,'In standard C (C99 and later), which of the following is guaranteed regarding sizeof(char)?','MCQ',NULL,1,4,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(5,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.168583','HARD','The expression 5 / 2 involves two integer literals, evaluating to integer 2. That integer is then converted to float 2.0f upon assignment. Misconception: Believing that assigning to a float variable forces the right-hand division to execute in floating-point.',1,'What will be printed by the following code on a system with standard IEEE 754 floats?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    float f = 5 / 2;\n    printf(\"%.2f\", f);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',NULL,1,5,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(6,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.170583','HARD','A variable qualified as const is read-only. The post-increment operator (x++) attempts to modify x, resulting in a compilation error. Misconception: Assuming const variables can be modified via post-increment or that the error is deferred to runtime.',1,'What happens when attempting to compile and execute the following C code?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    const int x = 10;\n    int y = x++;\n    printf(\"%d\", y);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',NULL,1,6,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(7,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.173583','EASY','Multiplication (*) has higher precedence than addition (+), so b * 2 evaluates first (20), followed by 5 + 20 = 25. Misconception: Evaluating strictly left-to-right yields (5 + 10) * 2 = 30.',1,'What is the value of \'c\' after executing this code snippet?\n\nint a = 5;\nint b = 10;\nint c = a + b * 2;','MCQ',NULL,2,7,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(8,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.176582','EASY','The single equals sign (=) assigns the value on the right to the variable on the left. The double equals sign (==) compares two values for equality, returning 1 (true) or 0 (false). Misconception: Accidental assignment inside if conditions (if (x = 5)) is a notorious beginner bug.',1,'What is the fundamental difference between \'x = 5\' and \'x == 5\' in C?','MCQ',NULL,2,8,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(9,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.179583','MEDIUM','++a is pre-increment: a becomes 6, then assigned to b (b=6). a++ is post-increment: current value of a (6) is assigned to c (c=6), then a increments to 7. Final values: a=7, b=6, c=6. Misconception: Confusing the timing of value evaluation between prefix and postfix increment.',1,'What is the output of the following C program?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int a = 5;\n    int b = ++a;\n    int c = a++;\n    printf(\"%d %d %d\", a, b, c);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',NULL,2,9,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(10,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.182582','MEDIUM','In a logical AND (&&) expression, if the left operand is false (0), the remaining operand is never evaluated (short-circuited). Since x is 0, ++y never runs, leaving y as 5. Misconception: Expecting all sub-expressions inside parentheses to always be executed.',1,'What will be printed by this code due to short-circuit evaluation?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int x = 0;\n    int y = 5;\n    if (x && ++y) {\n        y += 10;\n    }\n    printf(\"%d\", y);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',NULL,2,10,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(11,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.185583','HARD','The modulus operator returns the remainder of integer division. 17 divided by 5 is 3 with a remainder of 2 (5 * 3 + 2 = 17). Misconception: Mistaking % for percentage calculation or floating-point division.',1,'What does the modulus operator (%) do in C, and what is the result of \'17 % 5\'?','MCQ',NULL,2,11,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(12,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.188584','HARD','The condition (10 > 20) evaluates to false (0), so the expression resolves to the value after the colon, which is b (20). Misconception: Assuming ternary operators return boolean flags rather than evaluating the chosen branch value.',1,'What is the output of the following C snippet utilizing the conditional ternary operator?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int a = 10, b = 20;\n    int max = (a > b) ? a : b;\n    printf(\"%d\", max);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',NULL,2,12,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(13,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.191583','EASY','C passes function arguments by value. To allow scanf to write data into the caller\'s variable, it requires a pointer to the variable\'s memory address. Misconception: Believing & is an optional syntax or merely an integer indicator.',1,'Why does scanf require the address-of operator (&) for fundamental variables like \'scanf(\"%d\", &num);\'?','MCQ',NULL,3,13,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(14,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.193583','EASY','%c reads or writes a single character. %s is for null-terminated strings, and %d is for signed integers. Misconception: Using %s to read a single char, which can cause memory corruption because strings expect a null terminator.',1,'What is the format specifier used to read or print a single char in C?','MCQ',NULL,3,14,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(15,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.196582','MEDIUM','The specifier %.3f formats the number to 3 decimal places with standard mathematical rounding. Since the fourth digit is 5, 3.1415... rounds up to 3.142. Misconception: Assuming printf strictly truncates without rounding (expecting 3.141).',1,'What is the output of this printf statement?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    double pi = 3.14159265;\n    printf(\"%.3f\", pi);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',NULL,3,15,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(16,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.197583','MEDIUM','The %s format specifier in scanf stops reading at the first whitespace character (space, tab, newline). Therefore, str only contains \'Hello\'. fgets() must be used to read entire lines with spaces. Misconception: Assuming %s reads until the Enter key.',1,'When reading a string using \'scanf(\"%s\", str);\' where char str[50];, what happens if the user enters \'Hello World\'?','MCQ',NULL,3,16,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(17,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.200583','HARD','According to the C standard library specification, printf returns the total count of characters successfully written to the output stream. Misconception: Most beginners believe printf has a void return type or always returns 0 or 1.',1,'What value does printf return upon successful execution in C?','MCQ',NULL,3,17,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(18,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.202582','HARD','A number directly preceding the specifier designates the minimum field width. If the printed data is shorter than the width (42 is 2 characters), it is right-aligned and padded with leading spaces (3 spaces + 42). Misconception: Confusing minimum field width (%5d) with decimal precision (%.5d).',1,'Given this C snippet:\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int x = 42;\n    printf(\"%5d\", x);\n    return 0;\n}\n\nWhat does the format specifier \'%5d\' specify?','MCQ',NULL,3,18,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(19,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.205583','EASY','In C, integer 0 evaluates to false. ANY non-zero integer (including negative numbers like -1 or -100) evaluates to true. Misconception: Many students believe negative numbers evaluate to false.',1,'In C, which of the following values is evaluated as boolean FALSE in conditional expressions?','MCQ',NULL,4,19,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(20,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.207582','EASY','The statement if (x = 5) performs assignment, not comparison. The assignment expression evaluates to 5, which is non-zero (true), causing the if branch to execute. Misconception: Believing it tests whether x equals 5.',1,'What is printed by the following C code?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int x = 0;\n    if (x = 5) {\n        printf(\"True\");\n    } else {\n        printf(\"False\");\n    }\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',NULL,4,20,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(21,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.209583','MEDIUM','In C switch statements, execution enters at case 2 (\'Tue \') and falls through all subsequent cases because there are no break statements, printing \'Tue Wed End\'. Misconception: Forgetting that switch cases fall through automatically without explicit break.',1,'What is printed by this switch statement?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int day = 2;\n    switch (day) {\n        case 1: printf(\"Mon \");\n        case 2: printf(\"Tue \");\n        case 3: printf(\"Wed \");\n        default: printf(\"End\");\n    }\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',NULL,4,21,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(22,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.212582','MEDIUM','In C syntax, an else clause always associates with the nearest unmatched preceding if statement. Despite the misleading indentation, the else belongs to if (b > 0). Since a > 0 is true and b > 0 is false, it prints \'Y\'. Misconception: Relying on visual indentation instead of compiler parsing rules.',1,'Consider the classic dangling-else problem:\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int a = 1, b = 0;\n    if (a > 0)\n        if (b > 0)\n            printf(\"X\");\n    else\n        printf(\"Y\");\n    return 0;\n}\n\nWhat does this print?','MCQ',NULL,4,22,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(23,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.215583','HARD','Switch case labels in C must be compile-time integer constant expressions (such as char, int, or enum). Floating-point literals (like 2.5f) are explicitly prohibited. Misconception: Expecting switch statements to support floating-point values or arbitrary variables.',1,'Which of the following expressions CANNOT be used as a case label in a C switch statement?','MCQ',NULL,4,23,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(24,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.217582','HARD','The semicolon immediately following if (x > 5) acts as an empty null statement. The following block { printf(\"Hello\"); } is an independent compound statement that always executes regardless of the condition. Misconception: Not realizing that an accidental semicolon decouples the block from the if check.',1,'What is printed by the following code?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int x = 10;\n    if (x > 5)\n        ;\n    {\n        printf(\"Hello\");\n    }\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',NULL,4,24,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(25,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.219582','EASY','The loop variable i takes the sequential values 0, 1, 2, 3, and 4. When i reaches 5, the condition 5 < 5 is false, yielding exactly 5 iterations. Misconception: Off-by-one errors (thinking 0 to < 5 means 4 iterations).',1,'How many times does the body of this loop execute?\n\nint count = 0;\nfor (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {\n    count++;\n}','MCQ',NULL,5,25,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(26,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.222582','EASY','A while loop tests its condition before entering the body (entry-controlled), while a do-while loop evaluates its condition at the bottom (exit-controlled), guaranteeing at least one execution. Misconception: Assuming all loop types evaluate their condition before any statements execute.',1,'How is a do-while loop fundamentally different from a while loop in C?','MCQ',NULL,5,26,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(27,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.224583','MEDIUM','When i reaches 3, the break statement terminates the while loop immediately before printing 3 or incrementing i. Therefore, only \'1 2 \' are printed. Misconception: Confusing break (exits entire loop) with continue (skips to next iteration).',1,'What is printed by this loop?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int i = 1;\n    while (i <= 5) {\n        if (i == 3) {\n            break;\n        }\n        printf(\"%d \", i);\n        i++;\n    }\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',NULL,5,27,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(28,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.226583','MEDIUM','The loop iterates through i = 0, 1, 2, 3. When i is 2, continue skips sum += i. Thus sum = 0 + 1 + 3 = 4. Misconception: Adding all numbers (0+1+2+3=6) without accounting for the continue jump.',1,'What is the final value of sum in this snippet?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int sum = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {\n        if (i == 2) continue;\n        sum += i;\n    }\n    printf(\"%d\", sum);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',NULL,5,28,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(29,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.228583','HARD','Iteration 1: prints \'1 \', x becomes 3; condition (3 < 5) is true. Iteration 2: prints \'3 \', x becomes 5; condition (5 < 5) is false. Loop finishes. Output is \'1 3 \'. Misconception: Expecting 5 to be printed before the condition check terminates the loop.',1,'What will be printed by the following do-while loop?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int x = 1;\n    do {\n        printf(\"%d \", x);\n        x += 2;\n    } while (x < 5);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',NULL,5,29,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(30,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.231584','HARD','The loop increments c from 0 through 254 (255 iterations). When c becomes 255, the condition (255 < 255) is false, so it terminates after 255 iterations. (Note: if the condition were c <= 255, it would be an infinite loop because 255 + 1 wraps to 0). Misconception: Generalizing that any loop with unsigned char wrapping is automatically infinite.',1,'Consider this loop:\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    unsigned char c;\n    int iterations = 0;\n    for (c = 0; c < 255; c++) {\n        iterations++;\n    }\n    printf(\"%d\", iterations);\n    return 0;\n}\n\nWhat does this print?','MCQ',NULL,5,30,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(31,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.233583','EASY','C is strictly a pass-by-value language. Arguments passed into functions are copied; modifications to local parameter copies do not alter the caller\'s variables unless explicit pointers are passed. Misconception: Believing C supports pass-by-reference natively without pointers.',1,'In C, how are arguments passed to functions by default?','MCQ',NULL,6,31,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(32,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.235583','EASY','A function prototype declares the function\'s name, return type, and parameter list before its definition, enabling the compiler to validate argument types and counts at call sites. Misconception: Confusing a declaration with code generation or memory allocation.',1,'What is a function prototype in C and why is it used?','MCQ',NULL,6,32,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(33,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.237583','MEDIUM','Because parameters are passed by value, swap operates on isolated local copies of x and y. The original variables in main remain unchanged as \'10 20\'. Misconception: Believing a swap function without pointer dereferencing can mutate the caller\'s values.',1,'What is printed by this program?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nvoid swap(int a, int b) {\n    int temp = a;\n    a = b;\n    b = temp;\n}\nint main() {\n    int x = 10, y = 20;\n    swap(x, y);\n    printf(\"%d %d\", x, y);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',NULL,6,33,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(34,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.239582','MEDIUM','In a void function, \'return;\' serves as an early exit command, returning control immediately to the caller without supplying a value. Misconception: Believing void functions cannot contain any return statements or must return 0.',1,'What does the return statement do in a function whose return type is void?','MCQ',NULL,6,34,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(35,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.242583','HARD','A static local variable is initialized once and retains its value between successive function calls across the program\'s lifetime. Calling counter() 3 times increments count from 0 to 1, 2, and 3, printing \'1 2 3 \'. Misconception: Assuming local variables always re-initialize to 0 on every call.',1,'What is printed by the following code with a static local variable?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nvoid counter() {\n    static int count = 0;\n    count++;\n    printf(\"%d \", count);\n}\nint main() {\n    counter();\n    counter();\n    counter();\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',NULL,6,35,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(36,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.248582','HARD','This computes factorial: 4 * mystery(3) = 4 * 3 * mystery(2) = 4 * 3 * 2 * 1 = 24. Misconception: Confusing factorial multiplication with recursive addition (which would yield 10).',1,'What is the output of this recursive function?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint mystery(int n) {\n    if (n <= 1) return 1;\n    return n * mystery(n - 1);\n}\nint main() {\n    printf(\"%d\", mystery(4));\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',NULL,6,36,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(37,'2026-11-09 12:50:06.353440','MEDIUM','The text lists missing values, duplicate records, and inconsistent formatting as common data issues. Data encryption is not mentioned in the context of data quality problems.',1,'Which of the following is NOT a common data quality issue mentioned in the text?','MCQ',3,9,37,'AI','ACTIVE',1,3,NULL,3,NULL),(38,'2026-11-09 12:50:11.742738','EASY','Data cleaning is defined as the process of detecting incorrect, incomplete, inconsistent, or irrelevant data. This is explicitly stated in the section text.',1,'What is the primary purpose of data cleaning in the analytics pipeline?','MCQ',3,9,38,'AI','ACTIVE',1,3,NULL,3,NULL),(39,'2026-11-09 12:50:14.133601','EASY','The text explicitly states that Excel has built-in tools for cleaning and transformation as a key advantage.',1,'What is a key advantage of using Excel for data cleaning according to the text?','MCQ',3,9,39,'AI','ACTIVE',1,3,NULL,3,NULL),(40,'2026-11-09 12:50:15.002949','EASY','The TRIM() function removes extra spaces from text, as stated in the section text.',1,'Which Excel function is used to remove extra spaces from text?','MCQ',3,9,40,'AI','ACTIVE',1,3,NULL,3,NULL),(41,'2026-11-09 12:50:15.814407','EASY','The SUBSTITUTE() function replaces specific text within a string, as described in the section text.',1,'Which Excel function is used to replace specific text within a string?','MCQ',3,9,41,'AI','ACTIVE',1,3,NULL,3,NULL),(42,'2026-11-09 12:50:16.767501','MEDIUM','The IFERROR() function prevents formula errors when results are error types like #DIV/0! or #N/A, as explained in the section text.',1,'What is the purpose of the IFERROR() function in Excel?','MCQ',3,9,42,'AI','ACTIVE',1,3,NULL,3,NULL),(43,'2026-11-09 12:50:19.345742','EASY','The AVERAGEIF() function is designed to calculate the average of cells based on a single condition, such as averaging sales for a specific region.',1,'Which Excel function is used to calculate the average of sales data for a specific region?','MCQ',3,9,43,'AI','ACTIVE',1,3,NULL,3,NULL),(44,'2026-11-09 12:50:20.358044','EASY','The Text-to-Columns feature is used to split text into separate columns when there are delimiters present in the dataset.',1,'What is the primary purpose of the Text-to-Columns feature in Excel?','MCQ',3,9,44,'AI','ACTIVE',1,3,NULL,3,NULL),(45,'2026-11-09 12:50:21.529808','MEDIUM','Sturges\' Rule is applied in Step 2 to determine the ideal number of bins for a histogram.',1,'Which step involves calculating the ideal number of bins for a histogram using Sturges\' Rule?','MCQ',3,9,45,'AI','ACTIVE',1,3,NULL,3,NULL),(46,'2026-11-09 12:50:22.780107','EASY','The text explicitly states that to make a true histogram with no gaps between bars, the user must right-click any bar on the chart, select Format Data Series, and change the Gap Width to 0%.',1,'What is the correct step to create a histogram with no gaps between bars in Excel according to the provided text?','MCQ',3,9,46,'AI','ACTIVE',1,3,NULL,3,NULL),(47,'2026-11-09 12:59:32.278812','EASY','In C, %d or %i outputs a signed decimal integer. %f is for floating-point values, %c is for single characters, and %s is for null-terminated character strings. Misconception: Novices often mistakenly think %d stands for double.',1,'What is the correct format specifier to print a standard signed int variable in C using printf?','MCQ',3,10,47,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,5,NULL,NULL,NULL),(48,'2026-11-09 12:59:32.280813','EASY','In C, dividing two integers truncates any fractional portion toward zero. 7 / 2 produces 3, not 3.5. Misconception: Beginners assume the fractional part is preserved or rounded up.',1,'What is the value of \'result\' after executing this C code?\n\nint a = 7;\nint b = 2;\nint result = a / b;','MCQ',3,10,48,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,5,NULL,NULL,NULL),(49,'2026-11-09 12:59:32.282812','MEDIUM','Character literals in C are represented by integer ASCII values (\'A\' is 65). Adding 2 yields 67. Because the %d specifier is used, it prints the integer 67 instead of character \'C\'. Misconception: Expecting the letter \'C\' due to ignoring the %d integer specifier.',1,'What is printed by the following C program?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    char ch = \'A\';\n    printf(\"%d\", ch + 2);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,10,49,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,5,NULL,NULL,NULL),(50,'2026-11-09 12:59:32.284813','MEDIUM','By definition in the ISO C standard, sizeof(char) is always guaranteed to be exactly 1 byte. Misconception: Students familiar with Java assume char is 2 bytes for 16-bit Unicode.',1,'In standard C (C99 and later), which of the following is guaranteed regarding sizeof(char)?','MCQ',3,10,50,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,5,NULL,NULL,NULL),(51,'2026-11-09 12:59:32.287814','HARD','The expression 5 / 2 involves two integer literals, evaluating to integer 2. That integer is then converted to float 2.0f upon assignment. Misconception: Believing that assigning to a float variable forces the right-hand division to execute in floating-point.',1,'What will be printed by the following code on a system with standard IEEE 754 floats?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    float f = 5 / 2;\n    printf(\"%.2f\", f);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,10,51,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,5,NULL,NULL,NULL),(52,'2026-11-09 12:59:32.290812','HARD','A variable qualified as const is read-only. The post-increment operator (x++) attempts to modify x, resulting in a compilation error. Misconception: Assuming const variables can be modified via post-increment or that the error is deferred to runtime.',1,'What happens when attempting to compile and execute the following C code?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    const int x = 10;\n    int y = x++;\n    printf(\"%d\", y);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,10,52,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,5,NULL,NULL,NULL),(53,'2026-11-09 12:59:32.294812','EASY','Multiplication (*) has higher precedence than addition (+), so b * 2 evaluates first (20), followed by 5 + 20 = 25. Misconception: Evaluating strictly left-to-right yields (5 + 10) * 2 = 30.',1,'What is the value of \'c\' after executing this code snippet?\n\nint a = 5;\nint b = 10;\nint c = a + b * 2;','MCQ',3,11,53,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,5,NULL,NULL,NULL),(54,'2026-11-09 12:59:32.297812','EASY','The single equals sign (=) assigns the value on the right to the variable on the left. The double equals sign (==) compares two values for equality, returning 1 (true) or 0 (false). Misconception: Accidental assignment inside if conditions (if (x = 5)) is a notorious beginner bug.',1,'What is the fundamental difference between \'x = 5\' and \'x == 5\' in C?','MCQ',3,11,54,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,5,NULL,NULL,NULL),(55,'2026-11-09 12:59:32.299813','MEDIUM','++a is pre-increment: a becomes 6, then assigned to b (b=6). a++ is post-increment: current value of a (6) is assigned to c (c=6), then a increments to 7. Final values: a=7, b=6, c=6. Misconception: Confusing the timing of value evaluation between prefix and postfix increment.',1,'What is the output of the following C program?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int a = 5;\n    int b = ++a;\n    int c = a++;\n    printf(\"%d %d %d\", a, b, c);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,11,55,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,5,NULL,NULL,NULL),(56,'2026-11-09 12:59:32.302813','MEDIUM','In a logical AND (&&) expression, if the left operand is false (0), the remaining operand is never evaluated (short-circuited). Since x is 0, ++y never runs, leaving y as 5. Misconception: Expecting all sub-expressions inside parentheses to always be executed.',1,'What will be printed by this code due to short-circuit evaluation?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int x = 0;\n    int y = 5;\n    if (x && ++y) {\n        y += 10;\n    }\n    printf(\"%d\", y);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,11,56,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,5,NULL,NULL,NULL),(57,'2026-11-09 12:59:32.304812','HARD','The modulus operator returns the remainder of integer division. 17 divided by 5 is 3 with a remainder of 2 (5 * 3 + 2 = 17). Misconception: Mistaking % for percentage calculation or floating-point division.',1,'What does the modulus operator (%) do in C, and what is the result of \'17 % 5\'?','MCQ',3,11,57,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,5,NULL,NULL,NULL),(58,'2026-11-09 12:59:32.307812','HARD','The condition (10 > 20) evaluates to false (0), so the expression resolves to the value after the colon, which is b (20). Misconception: Assuming ternary operators return boolean flags rather than evaluating the chosen branch value.',1,'What is the output of the following C snippet utilizing the conditional ternary operator?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int a = 10, b = 20;\n    int max = (a > b) ? a : b;\n    printf(\"%d\", max);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,11,58,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,5,NULL,NULL,NULL),(59,'2026-11-09 12:59:32.309812','EASY','C passes function arguments by value. To allow scanf to write data into the caller\'s variable, it requires a pointer to the variable\'s memory address. Misconception: Believing & is an optional syntax or merely an integer indicator.',1,'Why does scanf require the address-of operator (&) for fundamental variables like \'scanf(\"%d\", &num);\'?','MCQ',3,12,59,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,5,NULL,NULL,NULL),(60,'2026-11-09 12:59:32.311812','EASY','%c reads or writes a single character. %s is for null-terminated strings, and %d is for signed integers. Misconception: Using %s to read a single char, which can cause memory corruption because strings expect a null terminator.',1,'What is the format specifier used to read or print a single char in C?','MCQ',3,12,60,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,5,NULL,NULL,NULL),(61,'2026-11-09 12:59:32.314812','MEDIUM','The specifier %.3f formats the number to 3 decimal places with standard mathematical rounding. Since the fourth digit is 5, 3.1415... rounds up to 3.142. Misconception: Assuming printf strictly truncates without rounding (expecting 3.141).',1,'What is the output of this printf statement?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    double pi = 3.14159265;\n    printf(\"%.3f\", pi);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,12,61,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,5,NULL,NULL,NULL),(62,'2026-11-09 12:59:32.316812','MEDIUM','The %s format specifier in scanf stops reading at the first whitespace character (space, tab, newline). Therefore, str only contains \'Hello\'. fgets() must be used to read entire lines with spaces. Misconception: Assuming %s reads until the Enter key.',1,'When reading a string using \'scanf(\"%s\", str);\' where char str[50];, what happens if the user enters \'Hello World\'?','MCQ',3,12,62,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,5,NULL,NULL,NULL),(63,'2026-11-09 12:59:32.318812','HARD','According to the C standard library specification, printf returns the total count of characters successfully written to the output stream. Misconception: Most beginners believe printf has a void return type or always returns 0 or 1.',1,'What value does printf return upon successful execution in C?','MCQ',3,12,63,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,5,NULL,NULL,NULL),(64,'2026-11-09 12:59:32.320812','HARD','A number directly preceding the specifier designates the minimum field width. If the printed data is shorter than the width (42 is 2 characters), it is right-aligned and padded with leading spaces (3 spaces + 42). Misconception: Confusing minimum field width (%5d) with decimal precision (%.5d).',1,'Given this C snippet:\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int x = 42;\n    printf(\"%5d\", x);\n    return 0;\n}\n\nWhat does the format specifier \'%5d\' specify?','MCQ',3,12,64,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,5,NULL,NULL,NULL),(65,'2026-11-09 12:59:32.321812','EASY','In C, integer 0 evaluates to false. ANY non-zero integer (including negative numbers like -1 or -100) evaluates to true. Misconception: Many students believe negative numbers evaluate to false.',1,'In C, which of the following values is evaluated as boolean FALSE in conditional expressions?','MCQ',3,13,65,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,5,NULL,NULL,NULL),(66,'2026-11-09 12:59:32.323812','EASY','The statement if (x = 5) performs assignment, not comparison. The assignment expression evaluates to 5, which is non-zero (true), causing the if branch to execute. Misconception: Believing it tests whether x equals 5.',1,'What is printed by the following C code?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int x = 0;\n    if (x = 5) {\n        printf(\"True\");\n    } else {\n        printf(\"False\");\n    }\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,13,66,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,5,NULL,NULL,NULL),(67,'2026-11-09 12:59:32.327813','MEDIUM','In C switch statements, execution enters at case 2 (\'Tue \') and falls through all subsequent cases because there are no break statements, printing \'Tue Wed End\'. Misconception: Forgetting that switch cases fall through automatically without explicit break.',1,'What is printed by this switch statement?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int day = 2;\n    switch (day) {\n        case 1: printf(\"Mon \");\n        case 2: printf(\"Tue \");\n        case 3: printf(\"Wed \");\n        default: printf(\"End\");\n    }\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,13,67,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,5,NULL,NULL,NULL),(68,'2026-11-09 12:59:32.332812','MEDIUM','In C syntax, an else clause always associates with the nearest unmatched preceding if statement. Despite the misleading indentation, the else belongs to if (b > 0). Since a > 0 is true and b > 0 is false, it prints \'Y\'. Misconception: Relying on visual indentation instead of compiler parsing rules.',1,'Consider the classic dangling-else problem:\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int a = 1, b = 0;\n    if (a > 0)\n        if (b > 0)\n            printf(\"X\");\n    else\n        printf(\"Y\");\n    return 0;\n}\n\nWhat does this print?','MCQ',3,13,68,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,5,NULL,NULL,NULL),(69,'2026-11-09 12:59:32.334813','HARD','Switch case labels in C must be compile-time integer constant expressions (such as char, int, or enum). Floating-point literals (like 2.5f) are explicitly prohibited. Misconception: Expecting switch statements to support floating-point values or arbitrary variables.',1,'Which of the following expressions CANNOT be used as a case label in a C switch statement?','MCQ',3,13,69,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,5,NULL,NULL,NULL),(70,'2026-11-09 12:59:32.336812','HARD','The semicolon immediately following if (x > 5) acts as an empty null statement. The following block { printf(\"Hello\"); } is an independent compound statement that always executes regardless of the condition. Misconception: Not realizing that an accidental semicolon decouples the block from the if check.',1,'What is printed by the following code?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int x = 10;\n    if (x > 5)\n        ;\n    {\n        printf(\"Hello\");\n    }\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,13,70,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,5,NULL,NULL,NULL),(71,'2026-11-09 12:59:32.339813','EASY','The loop variable i takes the sequential values 0, 1, 2, 3, and 4. When i reaches 5, the condition 5 < 5 is false, yielding exactly 5 iterations. Misconception: Off-by-one errors (thinking 0 to < 5 means 4 iterations).',1,'How many times does the body of this loop execute?\n\nint count = 0;\nfor (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {\n    count++;\n}','MCQ',3,14,71,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,5,NULL,NULL,NULL),(72,'2026-11-09 12:59:32.342813','EASY','A while loop tests its condition before entering the body (entry-controlled), while a do-while loop evaluates its condition at the bottom (exit-controlled), guaranteeing at least one execution. Misconception: Assuming all loop types evaluate their condition before any statements execute.',1,'How is a do-while loop fundamentally different from a while loop in C?','MCQ',3,14,72,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,5,NULL,NULL,NULL),(73,'2026-11-09 12:59:32.344812','MEDIUM','When i reaches 3, the break statement terminates the while loop immediately before printing 3 or incrementing i. Therefore, only \'1 2 \' are printed. Misconception: Confusing break (exits entire loop) with continue (skips to next iteration).',1,'What is printed by this loop?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int i = 1;\n    while (i <= 5) {\n        if (i == 3) {\n            break;\n        }\n        printf(\"%d \", i);\n        i++;\n    }\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,14,73,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,5,NULL,NULL,NULL),(74,'2026-11-09 12:59:32.346812','MEDIUM','The loop iterates through i = 0, 1, 2, 3. When i is 2, continue skips sum += i. Thus sum = 0 + 1 + 3 = 4. Misconception: Adding all numbers (0+1+2+3=6) without accounting for the continue jump.',1,'What is the final value of sum in this snippet?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int sum = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {\n        if (i == 2) continue;\n        sum += i;\n    }\n    printf(\"%d\", sum);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,14,74,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,5,NULL,NULL,NULL),(75,'2026-11-09 12:59:32.349813','HARD','Iteration 1: prints \'1 \', x becomes 3; condition (3 < 5) is true. Iteration 2: prints \'3 \', x becomes 5; condition (5 < 5) is false. Loop finishes. Output is \'1 3 \'. Misconception: Expecting 5 to be printed before the condition check terminates the loop.',1,'What will be printed by the following do-while loop?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int x = 1;\n    do {\n        printf(\"%d \", x);\n        x += 2;\n    } while (x < 5);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,14,75,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,5,NULL,NULL,NULL),(76,'2026-11-09 12:59:32.351812','HARD','The loop increments c from 0 through 254 (255 iterations). When c becomes 255, the condition (255 < 255) is false, so it terminates after 255 iterations. (Note: if the condition were c <= 255, it would be an infinite loop because 255 + 1 wraps to 0). Misconception: Generalizing that any loop with unsigned char wrapping is automatically infinite.',1,'Consider this loop:\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    unsigned char c;\n    int iterations = 0;\n    for (c = 0; c < 255; c++) {\n        iterations++;\n    }\n    printf(\"%d\", iterations);\n    return 0;\n}\n\nWhat does this print?','MCQ',3,14,76,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,5,NULL,NULL,NULL),(77,'2026-11-09 12:59:32.355813','EASY','C is strictly a pass-by-value language. Arguments passed into functions are copied; modifications to local parameter copies do not alter the caller\'s variables unless explicit pointers are passed. Misconception: Believing C supports pass-by-reference natively without pointers.',1,'In C, how are arguments passed to functions by default?','MCQ',3,15,77,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,5,NULL,NULL,NULL),(78,'2026-11-09 12:59:32.358812','EASY','A function prototype declares the function\'s name, return type, and parameter list before its definition, enabling the compiler to validate argument types and counts at call sites. Misconception: Confusing a declaration with code generation or memory allocation.',1,'What is a function prototype in C and why is it used?','MCQ',3,15,78,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,5,NULL,NULL,NULL),(79,'2026-11-09 12:59:32.360812','MEDIUM','Because parameters are passed by value, swap operates on isolated local copies of x and y. The original variables in main remain unchanged as \'10 20\'. Misconception: Believing a swap function without pointer dereferencing can mutate the caller\'s values.',1,'What is printed by this program?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nvoid swap(int a, int b) {\n    int temp = a;\n    a = b;\n    b = temp;\n}\nint main() {\n    int x = 10, y = 20;\n    swap(x, y);\n    printf(\"%d %d\", x, y);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,15,79,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,5,NULL,NULL,NULL),(80,'2026-11-09 12:59:32.362812','MEDIUM','In a void function, \'return;\' serves as an early exit command, returning control immediately to the caller without supplying a value. Misconception: Believing void functions cannot contain any return statements or must return 0.',1,'What does the return statement do in a function whose return type is void?','MCQ',3,15,80,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,5,NULL,NULL,NULL),(81,'2026-11-09 12:59:32.364813','HARD','A static local variable is initialized once and retains its value between successive function calls across the program\'s lifetime. Calling counter() 3 times increments count from 0 to 1, 2, and 3, printing \'1 2 3 \'. Misconception: Assuming local variables always re-initialize to 0 on every call.',1,'What is printed by the following code with a static local variable?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nvoid counter() {\n    static int count = 0;\n    count++;\n    printf(\"%d \", count);\n}\nint main() {\n    counter();\n    counter();\n    counter();\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,15,81,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,5,NULL,NULL,NULL),(82,'2026-11-09 12:59:32.366812','HARD','This computes factorial: 4 * mystery(3) = 4 * 3 * mystery(2) = 4 * 3 * 2 * 1 = 24. Misconception: Confusing factorial multiplication with recursive addition (which would yield 10).',1,'What is the output of this recursive function?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint mystery(int n) {\n    if (n <= 1) return 1;\n    return n * mystery(n - 1);\n}\nint main() {\n    printf(\"%d\", mystery(4));\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,15,82,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,5,NULL,NULL,NULL),(83,'2026-11-09 13:06:58.592320','EASY','Standard deviation is a measure of variability, not central tendency. Measures of central tendency include mean, median, and mode.',1,'Which of the following is NOT a measure of central tendency?','MCQ',3,16,83,'AI','ACTIVE',1,3,NULL,4,NULL),(84,'2026-11-09 13:07:06.985207','MEDIUM','Descriptive analytics helps summarize large datasets, describe patterns, trends, and distributions. It provides inputs for advanced analytics but does not predict future trends or perform hypothesis testing directly.',1,'What is the primary purpose of descriptive analytics?','MCQ',3,16,84,'AI','ACTIVE',1,3,NULL,4,NULL),(85,'2026-11-09 13:07:16.281403','MEDIUM','The median is the best measure of central tendency for ordinal data because it represents the middle value in an ordered dataset, which aligns with the nature of ordinal scales.',1,'Which measure of central tendency is most appropriate for ordinal data?','MCQ',3,16,85,'AI','ACTIVE',1,3,NULL,4,NULL),(86,'2026-11-09 13:07:38.579694','EASY','Studying distribution shape helps in identifying differences between distributions, describing them, identifying unusual observations, assessing assumptions, guiding analysis, and communicating dataset characteristics.',1,'What is the primary purpose of studying distribution shape in data analytics?','MCQ',3,16,86,'AI','ACTIVE',1,3,NULL,4,NULL),(87,'2026-11-09 13:07:47.238094','MEDIUM','Pearson’s Second Coefficient of Skewness uses the mode when it is known or can be estimated, as stated in the section text.',1,'Which formula is used for Pearson’s Second Coefficient of Skewness when the mode is known or can be estimated?','MCQ',3,16,87,'AI','ACTIVE',1,3,NULL,4,NULL),(88,'2026-11-09 13:07:52.031038','MEDIUM','Negative skewness indicates that the distribution has a longer or fatter tail on the left side, and the mean is typically less than the median.',1,'What does a negative skewness value indicate about a distribution?','MCQ',3,16,88,'AI','ACTIVE',1,3,NULL,4,NULL),(89,'2026-11-09 13:07:55.186291','EASY','The correct formula for the Lower Bound in the IQR Method is Q1 minus 1.5 times the IQR, as stated in the section text.',1,'What is the formula used to calculate the Lower Bound in the IQR Method?','MCQ',3,16,89,'AI','ACTIVE',1,3,NULL,4,NULL),(90,'2026-11-09 13:07:57.207452','EASY','The general rule for the z-score method is that a potential outlier is identified when the absolute value of the z-score exceeds 3, as mentioned in the section text.',1,'According to the z-score method, what is the general rule for identifying potential outliers?','MCQ',3,16,90,'AI','ACTIVE',1,3,NULL,4,NULL),(91,'2026-11-09 13:07:58.936559','MEDIUM','The IQR Method uses Q1 and Q3 to calculate the interquartile range and determine outliers based on the lower and upper bounds, as described in the section text.',1,'Which method uses both Q1 and Q3 to determine outliers?','MCQ',3,16,91,'AI','ACTIVE',1,3,NULL,4,NULL),(93,'2026-11-10 07:06:00.332688','EASY','The section explicitly states that data analytics involves examining raw data to uncover patterns, trends, relationships, and useful insights that can support decision-making.',1,'What is the primary purpose of data analytics according to the section text?','MCQ',3,22,93,'AI','ACTIVE',1,3,NULL,5,NULL),(94,'2026-11-10 07:06:02.170641','MEDIUM','The section text specifically mentions that the Business Intelligence Era (1990s–2000s) is characterized by the emergence of relational databases and data warehouses.',1,'Which era in the development of data analytics is characterized by the use of relational databases and data warehouses?','MCQ',3,22,94,'AI','ACTIVE',1,3,NULL,5,NULL),(95,'2026-11-10 07:06:03.878265','EASY','The section text clearly states that Predictive Analytics is used to forecast future outcomes and answers the question \'What is likely to happen?\'',1,'Which type of analytics answers the question \'What is likely to happen?\'?','MCQ',3,22,95,'AI','ACTIVE',1,3,NULL,5,NULL),(96,'2026-11-10 07:06:04.614467','MEDIUM','The section text explicitly lists COBOL as a technology used during the Early Data Processing Era (1960s–1980s).',1,'Which of the following technologies was used during the Early Data Processing Era (1960s–1980s)?','MCQ',3,22,96,'AI','ACTIVE',1,3,NULL,5,NULL),(97,'2026-11-10 07:06:05.674416','EASY','The section explicitly states that Step 1 involves \'clearly defining the business or research question\' and \'identifying objectives and success metrics\' with SMART criteria.',1,'What is the primary purpose of Step 1: Problem Definition in data analytics?','MCQ',3,22,97,'AI','ACTIVE',1,3,NULL,5,NULL),(98,'2026-11-10 07:06:06.399993','MEDIUM','The section directly mentions that \'Garbage In, Garbage Out (GIGO) – poor data leads to poor decisions\', emphasizing the importance of data accuracy during collection.',1,'Which of the following best describes the \'Garbage In, Garbage Out\' principle in data analytics?','MCQ',3,22,98,'AI','ACTIVE',1,3,NULL,5,NULL),(99,'2026-11-10 07:06:07.105417','MEDIUM','The section explicitly states that Big Data \'requires advanced tools like Hadoop, Spark, NoSQL databases\', while traditional data uses standard tools like SQL and Excel.',1,'What is a key characteristic that differentiates Big Data from traditional data?','MCQ',3,22,99,'AI','ACTIVE',1,3,NULL,5,NULL),(100,'2026-11-10 07:06:07.957691','EASY','The section clearly defines \'strategic decisions\' as those that involve higher-level issues and define the organization’s overall goals and aspirations for the future.',1,'Which type of decision-making in Step 7 involves defining an organization’s overall goals and aspirations for the future?','MCQ',3,22,100,'AI','ACTIVE',1,3,NULL,5,NULL),(101,'2026-11-10 07:06:09.192831','EASY','The example of a manufacturing plant optimizing production scheduling directly relates to improving operational efficiency by reducing downtime, which is explicitly mentioned in the section text.',1,'Which of the following is an example of how business analytics improves operational efficiency?','MCQ',3,22,101,'AI','ACTIVE',1,3,NULL,5,NULL),(102,'2026-11-10 07:06:10.922746','EASY','The section text explicitly states that demand forecasting helps reduce overstock and stockouts, which directly contributes to improved operational efficiency.',1,'What is a primary benefit of demand forecasting in inventory optimization?','MCQ',3,22,102,'AI','ACTIVE',1,3,NULL,5,NULL),(103,'2026-11-10 07:23:53.152997','EASY','The section text explicitly states that data serves as the foundation for information, analysis, and decision-making. This aligns with the first choice.',1,'What is the primary purpose of data in data analytics?','MCQ',3,23,103,'AI','ACTIVE',1,3,NULL,6,NULL),(104,'2026-11-10 07:24:10.866329','MEDIUM','The section text defines structured data as organized in rows and columns (tables) and provides examples such as sales records and customer tables.',1,'Which of the following is an example of structured data?','MCQ',3,23,104,'AI','ACTIVE',1,3,NULL,6,NULL),(105,'2026-11-10 07:24:15.469980','MEDIUM','The text clearly outlines that data is processed into information, which is then interpreted into knowledge. This directly corresponds to the first choice.',1,'What is the difference between data and information according to the text?','MCQ',3,23,105,'AI','ACTIVE',1,3,NULL,6,NULL),(106,'2026-11-10 07:24:21.322874','EASY','The section text explicitly states that secondary data sources are \'cost-effective and time-efficient, but less specific to the current research problem.\' This directly matches choice 3.',1,'Which of the following is a characteristic of secondary data sources?','MCQ',3,23,106,'AI','ACTIVE',1,3,NULL,6,NULL),(107,'2026-11-10 07:29:45.530662','MEDIUM','The section text states that Cochran\'s formula is used for estimating a population proportion when the population is large or treated as infinite. This matches choice 2.',1,'Which formula is appropriate when estimating a population proportion and the population size is large or treated as infinite?','MCQ',3,23,107,'AI','ACTIVE',1,3,NULL,6,NULL),(108,'2026-11-10 07:29:47.042810','EASY','The nominal level is characterized by categories without inherent order, such as gender and blood type. This is explicitly stated in the section text under \'Levels of Measurement - Nominal Level\'.',1,'Which level of measurement has categories with no inherent order and includes examples such as gender and blood type?','MCQ',3,23,108,'AI','ACTIVE',1,3,NULL,6,NULL),(109,'2026-11-10 07:29:48.144274','MEDIUM','The median and Spearman correlation are statistical methods suitable for ordinal data. The section text under \'Common Statistical Methods by Level\' specifies that the median is used for ordinal level data.',1,'Which statistical method is commonly used for data at the ordinal level of measurement?','MCQ',3,23,109,'AI','ACTIVE',1,3,NULL,6,NULL),(110,'2026-11-10 07:29:49.036708','MEDIUM','Data cleaning involves identifying and correcting errors, inconsistencies, and inaccuracies to ensure data accuracy and reliability. This is clearly outlined in the section text under \'Data Cleaning\'.',1,'What is a key objective of data cleaning in the data preparation process?','MCQ',3,23,110,'AI','ACTIVE',1,3,NULL,6,NULL),(111,'2026-11-10 07:29:50.562663','EASY','Feature scaling is used to standardize numeric variables so they are on a comparable scale, which prevents features with large ranges from dominating models and improves algorithm performance.',1,'What is the primary purpose of feature scaling in data analytics?','MCQ',3,23,111,'AI','ACTIVE',1,3,NULL,6,NULL),(112,'2026-11-10 07:30:05.892299','EASY','The section text defines internal data as \'generated within an organization\' and provides examples such as \'sales records, employee metrics.\' This aligns with choice 3.',1,'Which of the following is an example of internal data?','MCQ',3,23,112,'AI','ACTIVE',1,3,NULL,6,NULL),(113,'2026-11-10 12:33:57.644522','EASY','In C, %d or %i outputs a signed decimal integer. %f is for floating-point values, %c is for single characters, and %s is for null-terminated character strings. Misconception: Novices often mistakenly think %d stands for double.',1,'What is the correct format specifier to print a standard signed int variable in C using printf?','MCQ',3,25,113,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL),(114,'2026-11-10 12:33:57.651523','EASY','In C, dividing two integers truncates any fractional portion toward zero. 7 / 2 produces 3, not 3.5. Misconception: Beginners assume the fractional part is preserved or rounded up.',1,'What is the value of \'result\' after executing this C code?\n\nint a = 7;\nint b = 2;\nint result = a / b;','MCQ',3,25,114,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL),(115,'2026-11-10 12:33:57.653522','MEDIUM','Character literals in C are represented by integer ASCII values (\'A\' is 65). Adding 2 yields 67. Because the %d specifier is used, it prints the integer 67 instead of character \'C\'. Misconception: Expecting the letter \'C\' due to ignoring the %d integer specifier.',1,'What is printed by the following C program?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    char ch = \'A\';\n    printf(\"%d\", ch + 2);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,25,115,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL),(116,'2026-11-10 12:33:57.655522','MEDIUM','By definition in the ISO C standard, sizeof(char) is always guaranteed to be exactly 1 byte. Misconception: Students familiar with Java assume char is 2 bytes for 16-bit Unicode.',1,'In standard C (C99 and later), which of the following is guaranteed regarding sizeof(char)?','MCQ',3,25,116,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL),(117,'2026-11-10 12:33:57.658523','HARD','The expression 5 / 2 involves two integer literals, evaluating to integer 2. That integer is then converted to float 2.0f upon assignment. Misconception: Believing that assigning to a float variable forces the right-hand division to execute in floating-point.',1,'What will be printed by the following code on a system with standard IEEE 754 floats?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    float f = 5 / 2;\n    printf(\"%.2f\", f);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,25,117,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL),(118,'2026-11-10 12:33:57.661523','HARD','A variable qualified as const is read-only. The post-increment operator (x++) attempts to modify x, resulting in a compilation error. Misconception: Assuming const variables can be modified via post-increment or that the error is deferred to runtime.',1,'What happens when attempting to compile and execute the following C code?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    const int x = 10;\n    int y = x++;\n    printf(\"%d\", y);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,25,118,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL),(119,'2026-11-10 12:33:57.664522','EASY','Multiplication (*) has higher precedence than addition (+), so b * 2 evaluates first (20), followed by 5 + 20 = 25. Misconception: Evaluating strictly left-to-right yields (5 + 10) * 2 = 30.',1,'What is the value of \'c\' after executing this code snippet?\n\nint a = 5;\nint b = 10;\nint c = a + b * 2;','MCQ',3,26,119,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL),(120,'2026-11-10 12:33:57.666522','EASY','The single equals sign (=) assigns the value on the right to the variable on the left. The double equals sign (==) compares two values for equality, returning 1 (true) or 0 (false). Misconception: Accidental assignment inside if conditions (if (x = 5)) is a notorious beginner bug.',1,'What is the fundamental difference between \'x = 5\' and \'x == 5\' in C?','MCQ',3,26,120,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL),(121,'2026-11-10 12:33:57.668523','MEDIUM','++a is pre-increment: a becomes 6, then assigned to b (b=6). a++ is post-increment: current value of a (6) is assigned to c (c=6), then a increments to 7. Final values: a=7, b=6, c=6. Misconception: Confusing the timing of value evaluation between prefix and postfix increment.',1,'What is the output of the following C program?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int a = 5;\n    int b = ++a;\n    int c = a++;\n    printf(\"%d %d %d\", a, b, c);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,26,121,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL),(122,'2026-11-10 12:33:57.670522','MEDIUM','In a logical AND (&&) expression, if the left operand is false (0), the remaining operand is never evaluated (short-circuited). Since x is 0, ++y never runs, leaving y as 5. Misconception: Expecting all sub-expressions inside parentheses to always be executed.',1,'What will be printed by this code due to short-circuit evaluation?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int x = 0;\n    int y = 5;\n    if (x && ++y) {\n        y += 10;\n    }\n    printf(\"%d\", y);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,26,122,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL),(123,'2026-11-10 12:33:57.672522','HARD','The modulus operator returns the remainder of integer division. 17 divided by 5 is 3 with a remainder of 2 (5 * 3 + 2 = 17). Misconception: Mistaking % for percentage calculation or floating-point division.',1,'What does the modulus operator (%) do in C, and what is the result of \'17 % 5\'?','MCQ',3,26,123,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL),(124,'2026-11-10 12:33:57.676523','HARD','The condition (10 > 20) evaluates to false (0), so the expression resolves to the value after the colon, which is b (20). Misconception: Assuming ternary operators return boolean flags rather than evaluating the chosen branch value.',1,'What is the output of the following C snippet utilizing the conditional ternary operator?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int a = 10, b = 20;\n    int max = (a > b) ? a : b;\n    printf(\"%d\", max);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,26,124,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL),(125,'2026-11-10 12:33:57.681086','EASY','C passes function arguments by value. To allow scanf to write data into the caller\'s variable, it requires a pointer to the variable\'s memory address. Misconception: Believing & is an optional syntax or merely an integer indicator.',1,'Why does scanf require the address-of operator (&) for fundamental variables like \'scanf(\"%d\", &num);\'?','MCQ',3,27,125,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL),(126,'2026-11-10 12:33:57.685088','EASY','%c reads or writes a single character. %s is for null-terminated strings, and %d is for signed integers. Misconception: Using %s to read a single char, which can cause memory corruption because strings expect a null terminator.',1,'What is the format specifier used to read or print a single char in C?','MCQ',3,27,126,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL),(127,'2026-11-10 12:33:57.688091','MEDIUM','The specifier %.3f formats the number to 3 decimal places with standard mathematical rounding. Since the fourth digit is 5, 3.1415... rounds up to 3.142. Misconception: Assuming printf strictly truncates without rounding (expecting 3.141).',1,'What is the output of this printf statement?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    double pi = 3.14159265;\n    printf(\"%.3f\", pi);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,27,127,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL),(128,'2026-11-10 12:33:57.690088','MEDIUM','The %s format specifier in scanf stops reading at the first whitespace character (space, tab, newline). Therefore, str only contains \'Hello\'. fgets() must be used to read entire lines with spaces. Misconception: Assuming %s reads until the Enter key.',1,'When reading a string using \'scanf(\"%s\", str);\' where char str[50];, what happens if the user enters \'Hello World\'?','MCQ',3,27,128,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL),(129,'2026-11-10 12:33:57.692088','HARD','According to the C standard library specification, printf returns the total count of characters successfully written to the output stream. Misconception: Most beginners believe printf has a void return type or always returns 0 or 1.',1,'What value does printf return upon successful execution in C?','MCQ',3,27,129,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL),(130,'2026-11-10 12:33:57.694089','HARD','A number directly preceding the specifier designates the minimum field width. If the printed data is shorter than the width (42 is 2 characters), it is right-aligned and padded with leading spaces (3 spaces + 42). Misconception: Confusing minimum field width (%5d) with decimal precision (%.5d).',1,'Given this C snippet:\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int x = 42;\n    printf(\"%5d\", x);\n    return 0;\n}\n\nWhat does the format specifier \'%5d\' specify?','MCQ',3,27,130,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL),(131,'2026-11-10 12:33:57.697150','EASY','In C, integer 0 evaluates to false. ANY non-zero integer (including negative numbers like -1 or -100) evaluates to true. Misconception: Many students believe negative numbers evaluate to false.',1,'In C, which of the following values is evaluated as boolean FALSE in conditional expressions?','MCQ',3,28,131,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL),(132,'2026-11-10 12:33:57.699148','EASY','The statement if (x = 5) performs assignment, not comparison. The assignment expression evaluates to 5, which is non-zero (true), causing the if branch to execute. Misconception: Believing it tests whether x equals 5.',1,'What is printed by the following C code?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int x = 0;\n    if (x = 5) {\n        printf(\"True\");\n    } else {\n        printf(\"False\");\n    }\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,28,132,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL),(133,'2026-11-10 12:33:57.702149','MEDIUM','In C switch statements, execution enters at case 2 (\'Tue \') and falls through all subsequent cases because there are no break statements, printing \'Tue Wed End\'. Misconception: Forgetting that switch cases fall through automatically without explicit break.',1,'What is printed by this switch statement?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int day = 2;\n    switch (day) {\n        case 1: printf(\"Mon \");\n        case 2: printf(\"Tue \");\n        case 3: printf(\"Wed \");\n        default: printf(\"End\");\n    }\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,28,133,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL),(134,'2026-11-10 12:33:57.705149','MEDIUM','In C syntax, an else clause always associates with the nearest unmatched preceding if statement. Despite the misleading indentation, the else belongs to if (b > 0). Since a > 0 is true and b > 0 is false, it prints \'Y\'. Misconception: Relying on visual indentation instead of compiler parsing rules.',1,'Consider the classic dangling-else problem:\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int a = 1, b = 0;\n    if (a > 0)\n        if (b > 0)\n            printf(\"X\");\n    else\n        printf(\"Y\");\n    return 0;\n}\n\nWhat does this print?','MCQ',3,28,134,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL),(135,'2026-11-10 12:33:57.707148','HARD','Switch case labels in C must be compile-time integer constant expressions (such as char, int, or enum). Floating-point literals (like 2.5f) are explicitly prohibited. Misconception: Expecting switch statements to support floating-point values or arbitrary variables.',1,'Which of the following expressions CANNOT be used as a case label in a C switch statement?','MCQ',3,28,135,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL),(136,'2026-11-10 12:33:57.709148','HARD','The semicolon immediately following if (x > 5) acts as an empty null statement. The following block { printf(\"Hello\"); } is an independent compound statement that always executes regardless of the condition. Misconception: Not realizing that an accidental semicolon decouples the block from the if check.',1,'What is printed by the following code?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int x = 10;\n    if (x > 5)\n        ;\n    {\n        printf(\"Hello\");\n    }\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,28,136,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL),(137,'2026-11-10 12:33:57.711148','EASY','The loop variable i takes the sequential values 0, 1, 2, 3, and 4. When i reaches 5, the condition 5 < 5 is false, yielding exactly 5 iterations. Misconception: Off-by-one errors (thinking 0 to < 5 means 4 iterations).',1,'How many times does the body of this loop execute?\n\nint count = 0;\nfor (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {\n    count++;\n}','MCQ',3,29,137,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL),(138,'2026-11-10 12:33:57.713692','EASY','A while loop tests its condition before entering the body (entry-controlled), while a do-while loop evaluates its condition at the bottom (exit-controlled), guaranteeing at least one execution. Misconception: Assuming all loop types evaluate their condition before any statements execute.',1,'How is a do-while loop fundamentally different from a while loop in C?','MCQ',3,29,138,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL),(139,'2026-11-10 12:33:57.716692','MEDIUM','When i reaches 3, the break statement terminates the while loop immediately before printing 3 or incrementing i. Therefore, only \'1 2 \' are printed. Misconception: Confusing break (exits entire loop) with continue (skips to next iteration).',1,'What is printed by this loop?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int i = 1;\n    while (i <= 5) {\n        if (i == 3) {\n            break;\n        }\n        printf(\"%d \", i);\n        i++;\n    }\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,29,139,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL),(140,'2026-11-10 12:33:57.718692','MEDIUM','The loop iterates through i = 0, 1, 2, 3. When i is 2, continue skips sum += i. Thus sum = 0 + 1 + 3 = 4. Misconception: Adding all numbers (0+1+2+3=6) without accounting for the continue jump.',1,'What is the final value of sum in this snippet?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int sum = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {\n        if (i == 2) continue;\n        sum += i;\n    }\n    printf(\"%d\", sum);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,29,140,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL),(141,'2026-11-10 12:33:57.721692','HARD','Iteration 1: prints \'1 \', x becomes 3; condition (3 < 5) is true. Iteration 2: prints \'3 \', x becomes 5; condition (5 < 5) is false. Loop finishes. Output is \'1 3 \'. Misconception: Expecting 5 to be printed before the condition check terminates the loop.',1,'What will be printed by the following do-while loop?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int x = 1;\n    do {\n        printf(\"%d \", x);\n        x += 2;\n    } while (x < 5);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,29,141,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL),(142,'2026-11-10 12:33:57.723692','HARD','The loop increments c from 0 through 254 (255 iterations). When c becomes 255, the condition (255 < 255) is false, so it terminates after 255 iterations. (Note: if the condition were c <= 255, it would be an infinite loop because 255 + 1 wraps to 0). Misconception: Generalizing that any loop with unsigned char wrapping is automatically infinite.',1,'Consider this loop:\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    unsigned char c;\n    int iterations = 0;\n    for (c = 0; c < 255; c++) {\n        iterations++;\n    }\n    printf(\"%d\", iterations);\n    return 0;\n}\n\nWhat does this print?','MCQ',3,29,142,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL),(143,'2026-11-10 12:33:57.726692','EASY','C is strictly a pass-by-value language. Arguments passed into functions are copied; modifications to local parameter copies do not alter the caller\'s variables unless explicit pointers are passed. Misconception: Believing C supports pass-by-reference natively without pointers.',1,'In C, how are arguments passed to functions by default?','MCQ',3,30,143,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL),(144,'2026-11-10 12:33:57.729692','EASY','A function prototype declares the function\'s name, return type, and parameter list before its definition, enabling the compiler to validate argument types and counts at call sites. Misconception: Confusing a declaration with code generation or memory allocation.',1,'What is a function prototype in C and why is it used?','MCQ',3,30,144,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL),(145,'2026-11-10 12:33:57.732692','MEDIUM','Because parameters are passed by value, swap operates on isolated local copies of x and y. The original variables in main remain unchanged as \'10 20\'. Misconception: Believing a swap function without pointer dereferencing can mutate the caller\'s values.',1,'What is printed by this program?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nvoid swap(int a, int b) {\n    int temp = a;\n    a = b;\n    b = temp;\n}\nint main() {\n    int x = 10, y = 20;\n    swap(x, y);\n    printf(\"%d %d\", x, y);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,30,145,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL),(146,'2026-11-10 12:33:57.734692','MEDIUM','In a void function, \'return;\' serves as an early exit command, returning control immediately to the caller without supplying a value. Misconception: Believing void functions cannot contain any return statements or must return 0.',1,'What does the return statement do in a function whose return type is void?','MCQ',3,30,146,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL),(147,'2026-11-10 12:33:57.736692','HARD','A static local variable is initialized once and retains its value between successive function calls across the program\'s lifetime. Calling counter() 3 times increments count from 0 to 1, 2, and 3, printing \'1 2 3 \'. Misconception: Assuming local variables always re-initialize to 0 on every call.',1,'What is printed by the following code with a static local variable?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nvoid counter() {\n    static int count = 0;\n    count++;\n    printf(\"%d \", count);\n}\nint main() {\n    counter();\n    counter();\n    counter();\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,30,147,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL),(148,'2026-11-10 12:33:57.738692','HARD','This computes factorial: 4 * mystery(3) = 4 * 3 * mystery(2) = 4 * 3 * 2 * 1 = 24. Misconception: Confusing factorial multiplication with recursive addition (which would yield 10).',1,'What is the output of this recursive function?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint mystery(int n) {\n    if (n <= 1) return 1;\n    return n * mystery(n - 1);\n}\nint main() {\n    printf(\"%d\", mystery(4));\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,30,148,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL),(149,'2026-11-13 18:02:15.396819','EASY','char holds a single 1-byte character (such as an ASCII character).',2,'Which C data type is typically used to store a single ASCII character?','MULTIPLE_CHOICE',3,1,149,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(150,'2026-11-13 18:02:15.398183','EASY','Standard input/output functions printf and scanf are declared in stdio.h.',2,'What standard C header file must be included to use printf() and scanf()?','ENUMERATION',3,3,150,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(151,'2026-11-13 18:02:15.399087','MEDIUM','A do-while loop checks its condition after the loop body, so it always executes at least once.',2,'How many times does a standard do-while loop execute its body at minimum?','ENUMERATION',3,5,151,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(152,'2026-11-13 18:02:15.399568','HARD','Model note: switch requires integral/enum constant expressions and break to prevent fall-through; if-else supports arbitrary boolean expressions and ranges.',10,'Explain the difference between an if-else chain and a switch statement in C, including when you would choose one over the other.','ESSAY',3,4,152,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,1,NULL,NULL,NULL),(153,'2026-11-13 18:02:15.413694','EASY','Diagnostic analytics investigates past data to explain why an outcome occurred.',2,'Which type of analytics focuses on understanding why something happened in the past?','MULTIPLE_CHOICE',3,22,153,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,3,NULL,NULL,NULL),(154,'2026-11-13 18:02:15.414309','EASY','CSV stands for Comma-Separated Values.',2,'What does the acronym CSV stand for in data processing?','ENUMERATION',3,22,154,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,3,NULL,NULL,NULL),(155,'2026-11-13 18:02:15.414717','MEDIUM','Model note: Line charts show trends across continuous time; bar charts compare discrete categories. Mention clear labels and non-misleading axes.',10,'Describe how you would choose between a bar chart and a line chart when presenting quarterly sales data to executives.','ESSAY',3,24,155,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,3,NULL,NULL,NULL),(156,'2026-11-14 14:43:27.707404','EASY','In C, %d or %i outputs a signed decimal integer. %f is for floating-point values, %c is for single characters, and %s is for null-terminated character strings. Misconception: Novices often mistakenly think %d stands for double.',1,'What is the correct format specifier to print a standard signed int variable in C using printf?','MCQ',3,32,156,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,9,NULL,NULL,NULL),(157,'2026-11-14 14:43:27.717404','EASY','In C, dividing two integers truncates any fractional portion toward zero. 7 / 2 produces 3, not 3.5. Misconception: Beginners assume the fractional part is preserved or rounded up.',1,'What is the value of \'result\' after executing this C code?\n\nint a = 7;\nint b = 2;\nint result = a / b;','MCQ',3,32,157,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,9,NULL,NULL,NULL),(158,'2026-11-14 14:43:27.719404','MEDIUM','Character literals in C are represented by integer ASCII values (\'A\' is 65). Adding 2 yields 67. Because the %d specifier is used, it prints the integer 67 instead of character \'C\'. Misconception: Expecting the letter \'C\' due to ignoring the %d integer specifier.',1,'What is printed by the following C program?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    char ch = \'A\';\n    printf(\"%d\", ch + 2);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,32,158,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,9,NULL,NULL,NULL),(159,'2026-11-14 14:43:27.725404','MEDIUM','By definition in the ISO C standard, sizeof(char) is always guaranteed to be exactly 1 byte. Misconception: Students familiar with Java assume char is 2 bytes for 16-bit Unicode.',1,'In standard C (C99 and later), which of the following is guaranteed regarding sizeof(char)?','MCQ',3,32,159,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,9,NULL,NULL,NULL),(160,'2026-11-14 14:43:27.729404','HARD','The expression 5 / 2 involves two integer literals, evaluating to integer 2. That integer is then converted to float 2.0f upon assignment. Misconception: Believing that assigning to a float variable forces the right-hand division to execute in floating-point.',1,'What will be printed by the following code on a system with standard IEEE 754 floats?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    float f = 5 / 2;\n    printf(\"%.2f\", f);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,32,160,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,9,NULL,NULL,NULL),(161,'2026-11-14 14:43:27.731405','HARD','A variable qualified as const is read-only. The post-increment operator (x++) attempts to modify x, resulting in a compilation error. Misconception: Assuming const variables can be modified via post-increment or that the error is deferred to runtime.',1,'What happens when attempting to compile and execute the following C code?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    const int x = 10;\n    int y = x++;\n    printf(\"%d\", y);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,32,161,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,9,NULL,NULL,NULL),(162,'2026-11-14 14:43:27.734404','EASY','Multiplication (*) has higher precedence than addition (+), so b * 2 evaluates first (20), followed by 5 + 20 = 25. Misconception: Evaluating strictly left-to-right yields (5 + 10) * 2 = 30.',1,'What is the value of \'c\' after executing this code snippet?\n\nint a = 5;\nint b = 10;\nint c = a + b * 2;','MCQ',3,33,162,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,9,NULL,NULL,NULL),(163,'2026-11-14 14:43:27.736404','EASY','The single equals sign (=) assigns the value on the right to the variable on the left. The double equals sign (==) compares two values for equality, returning 1 (true) or 0 (false). Misconception: Accidental assignment inside if conditions (if (x = 5)) is a notorious beginner bug.',1,'What is the fundamental difference between \'x = 5\' and \'x == 5\' in C?','MCQ',3,33,163,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,9,NULL,NULL,NULL),(164,'2026-11-14 14:43:27.738404','MEDIUM','++a is pre-increment: a becomes 6, then assigned to b (b=6). a++ is post-increment: current value of a (6) is assigned to c (c=6), then a increments to 7. Final values: a=7, b=6, c=6. Misconception: Confusing the timing of value evaluation between prefix and postfix increment.',1,'What is the output of the following C program?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int a = 5;\n    int b = ++a;\n    int c = a++;\n    printf(\"%d %d %d\", a, b, c);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,33,164,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,9,NULL,NULL,NULL),(165,'2026-11-14 14:43:27.741404','MEDIUM','In a logical AND (&&) expression, if the left operand is false (0), the remaining operand is never evaluated (short-circuited). Since x is 0, ++y never runs, leaving y as 5. Misconception: Expecting all sub-expressions inside parentheses to always be executed.',1,'What will be printed by this code due to short-circuit evaluation?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int x = 0;\n    int y = 5;\n    if (x && ++y) {\n        y += 10;\n    }\n    printf(\"%d\", y);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,33,165,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,9,NULL,NULL,NULL),(166,'2026-11-14 14:43:27.743405','HARD','The modulus operator returns the remainder of integer division. 17 divided by 5 is 3 with a remainder of 2 (5 * 3 + 2 = 17). Misconception: Mistaking % for percentage calculation or floating-point division.',1,'What does the modulus operator (%) do in C, and what is the result of \'17 % 5\'?','MCQ',3,33,166,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,9,NULL,NULL,NULL),(167,'2026-11-14 14:43:27.745405','HARD','The condition (10 > 20) evaluates to false (0), so the expression resolves to the value after the colon, which is b (20). Misconception: Assuming ternary operators return boolean flags rather than evaluating the chosen branch value.',1,'What is the output of the following C snippet utilizing the conditional ternary operator?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int a = 10, b = 20;\n    int max = (a > b) ? a : b;\n    printf(\"%d\", max);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,33,167,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,9,NULL,NULL,NULL),(168,'2026-11-14 14:43:27.748405','EASY','C passes function arguments by value. To allow scanf to write data into the caller\'s variable, it requires a pointer to the variable\'s memory address. Misconception: Believing & is an optional syntax or merely an integer indicator.',1,'Why does scanf require the address-of operator (&) for fundamental variables like \'scanf(\"%d\", &num);\'?','MCQ',3,34,168,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,9,NULL,NULL,NULL),(169,'2026-11-14 14:43:27.750405','EASY','%c reads or writes a single character. %s is for null-terminated strings, and %d is for signed integers. Misconception: Using %s to read a single char, which can cause memory corruption because strings expect a null terminator.',1,'What is the format specifier used to read or print a single char in C?','MCQ',3,34,169,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,9,NULL,NULL,NULL),(170,'2026-11-14 14:43:27.753405','MEDIUM','The specifier %.3f formats the number to 3 decimal places with standard mathematical rounding. Since the fourth digit is 5, 3.1415... rounds up to 3.142. Misconception: Assuming printf strictly truncates without rounding (expecting 3.141).',1,'What is the output of this printf statement?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    double pi = 3.14159265;\n    printf(\"%.3f\", pi);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,34,170,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,9,NULL,NULL,NULL),(171,'2026-11-14 14:43:27.755404','MEDIUM','The %s format specifier in scanf stops reading at the first whitespace character (space, tab, newline). Therefore, str only contains \'Hello\'. fgets() must be used to read entire lines with spaces. Misconception: Assuming %s reads until the Enter key.',1,'When reading a string using \'scanf(\"%s\", str);\' where char str[50];, what happens if the user enters \'Hello World\'?','MCQ',3,34,171,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,9,NULL,NULL,NULL),(172,'2026-11-14 14:43:27.758404','HARD','According to the C standard library specification, printf returns the total count of characters successfully written to the output stream. Misconception: Most beginners believe printf has a void return type or always returns 0 or 1.',1,'What value does printf return upon successful execution in C?','MCQ',3,34,172,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,9,NULL,NULL,NULL),(173,'2026-11-14 14:43:27.759404','HARD','A number directly preceding the specifier designates the minimum field width. If the printed data is shorter than the width (42 is 2 characters), it is right-aligned and padded with leading spaces (3 spaces + 42). Misconception: Confusing minimum field width (%5d) with decimal precision (%.5d).',1,'Given this C snippet:\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int x = 42;\n    printf(\"%5d\", x);\n    return 0;\n}\n\nWhat does the format specifier \'%5d\' specify?','MCQ',3,34,173,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,9,NULL,NULL,NULL),(174,'2026-11-14 14:43:27.762404','EASY','In C, integer 0 evaluates to false. ANY non-zero integer (including negative numbers like -1 or -100) evaluates to true. Misconception: Many students believe negative numbers evaluate to false.',1,'In C, which of the following values is evaluated as boolean FALSE in conditional expressions?','MCQ',3,35,174,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,9,NULL,NULL,NULL),(175,'2026-11-14 14:43:27.764404','EASY','The statement if (x = 5) performs assignment, not comparison. The assignment expression evaluates to 5, which is non-zero (true), causing the if branch to execute. Misconception: Believing it tests whether x equals 5.',1,'What is printed by the following C code?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int x = 0;\n    if (x = 5) {\n        printf(\"True\");\n    } else {\n        printf(\"False\");\n    }\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,35,175,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,9,NULL,NULL,NULL),(176,'2026-11-14 14:43:27.766405','MEDIUM','In C switch statements, execution enters at case 2 (\'Tue \') and falls through all subsequent cases because there are no break statements, printing \'Tue Wed End\'. Misconception: Forgetting that switch cases fall through automatically without explicit break.',1,'What is printed by this switch statement?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int day = 2;\n    switch (day) {\n        case 1: printf(\"Mon \");\n        case 2: printf(\"Tue \");\n        case 3: printf(\"Wed \");\n        default: printf(\"End\");\n    }\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,35,176,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,9,NULL,NULL,NULL),(177,'2026-11-14 14:43:27.769406','MEDIUM','In C syntax, an else clause always associates with the nearest unmatched preceding if statement. Despite the misleading indentation, the else belongs to if (b > 0). Since a > 0 is true and b > 0 is false, it prints \'Y\'. Misconception: Relying on visual indentation instead of compiler parsing rules.',1,'Consider the classic dangling-else problem:\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int a = 1, b = 0;\n    if (a > 0)\n        if (b > 0)\n            printf(\"X\");\n    else\n        printf(\"Y\");\n    return 0;\n}\n\nWhat does this print?','MCQ',3,35,177,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,9,NULL,NULL,NULL),(178,'2026-11-14 14:43:27.772404','HARD','Switch case labels in C must be compile-time integer constant expressions (such as char, int, or enum). Floating-point literals (like 2.5f) are explicitly prohibited. Misconception: Expecting switch statements to support floating-point values or arbitrary variables.',1,'Which of the following expressions CANNOT be used as a case label in a C switch statement?','MCQ',3,35,178,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,9,NULL,NULL,NULL),(179,'2026-11-14 14:43:27.774404','HARD','The semicolon immediately following if (x > 5) acts as an empty null statement. The following block { printf(\"Hello\"); } is an independent compound statement that always executes regardless of the condition. Misconception: Not realizing that an accidental semicolon decouples the block from the if check.',1,'What is printed by the following code?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int x = 10;\n    if (x > 5)\n        ;\n    {\n        printf(\"Hello\");\n    }\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,35,179,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,9,NULL,NULL,NULL),(180,'2026-11-14 14:43:27.783404','EASY','The loop variable i takes the sequential values 0, 1, 2, 3, and 4. When i reaches 5, the condition 5 < 5 is false, yielding exactly 5 iterations. Misconception: Off-by-one errors (thinking 0 to < 5 means 4 iterations).',1,'How many times does the body of this loop execute?\n\nint count = 0;\nfor (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {\n    count++;\n}','MCQ',3,36,180,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,9,NULL,NULL,NULL),(181,'2026-11-14 14:43:27.786404','EASY','A while loop tests its condition before entering the body (entry-controlled), while a do-while loop evaluates its condition at the bottom (exit-controlled), guaranteeing at least one execution. Misconception: Assuming all loop types evaluate their condition before any statements execute.',1,'How is a do-while loop fundamentally different from a while loop in C?','MCQ',3,36,181,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,9,NULL,NULL,NULL),(182,'2026-11-14 14:43:27.788404','MEDIUM','When i reaches 3, the break statement terminates the while loop immediately before printing 3 or incrementing i. Therefore, only \'1 2 \' are printed. Misconception: Confusing break (exits entire loop) with continue (skips to next iteration).',1,'What is printed by this loop?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int i = 1;\n    while (i <= 5) {\n        if (i == 3) {\n            break;\n        }\n        printf(\"%d \", i);\n        i++;\n    }\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,36,182,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,9,NULL,NULL,NULL),(183,'2026-11-14 14:43:27.790404','MEDIUM','The loop iterates through i = 0, 1, 2, 3. When i is 2, continue skips sum += i. Thus sum = 0 + 1 + 3 = 4. Misconception: Adding all numbers (0+1+2+3=6) without accounting for the continue jump.',1,'What is the final value of sum in this snippet?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int sum = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {\n        if (i == 2) continue;\n        sum += i;\n    }\n    printf(\"%d\", sum);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,36,183,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,9,NULL,NULL,NULL),(184,'2026-11-14 14:43:27.794404','HARD','Iteration 1: prints \'1 \', x becomes 3; condition (3 < 5) is true. Iteration 2: prints \'3 \', x becomes 5; condition (5 < 5) is false. Loop finishes. Output is \'1 3 \'. Misconception: Expecting 5 to be printed before the condition check terminates the loop.',1,'What will be printed by the following do-while loop?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int x = 1;\n    do {\n        printf(\"%d \", x);\n        x += 2;\n    } while (x < 5);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,36,184,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,9,NULL,NULL,NULL),(185,'2026-11-14 14:43:27.797405','HARD','The loop increments c from 0 through 254 (255 iterations). When c becomes 255, the condition (255 < 255) is false, so it terminates after 255 iterations. (Note: if the condition were c <= 255, it would be an infinite loop because 255 + 1 wraps to 0). Misconception: Generalizing that any loop with unsigned char wrapping is automatically infinite.',1,'Consider this loop:\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    unsigned char c;\n    int iterations = 0;\n    for (c = 0; c < 255; c++) {\n        iterations++;\n    }\n    printf(\"%d\", iterations);\n    return 0;\n}\n\nWhat does this print?','MCQ',3,36,185,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,9,NULL,NULL,NULL),(186,'2026-11-14 14:43:27.799404','EASY','C is strictly a pass-by-value language. Arguments passed into functions are copied; modifications to local parameter copies do not alter the caller\'s variables unless explicit pointers are passed. Misconception: Believing C supports pass-by-reference natively without pointers.',1,'In C, how are arguments passed to functions by default?','MCQ',3,37,186,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,9,NULL,NULL,NULL),(187,'2026-11-14 14:43:27.801404','EASY','A function prototype declares the function\'s name, return type, and parameter list before its definition, enabling the compiler to validate argument types and counts at call sites. Misconception: Confusing a declaration with code generation or memory allocation.',1,'What is a function prototype in C and why is it used?','MCQ',3,37,187,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,9,NULL,NULL,NULL),(188,'2026-11-14 14:43:27.803405','MEDIUM','Because parameters are passed by value, swap operates on isolated local copies of x and y. The original variables in main remain unchanged as \'10 20\'. Misconception: Believing a swap function without pointer dereferencing can mutate the caller\'s values.',1,'What is printed by this program?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nvoid swap(int a, int b) {\n    int temp = a;\n    a = b;\n    b = temp;\n}\nint main() {\n    int x = 10, y = 20;\n    swap(x, y);\n    printf(\"%d %d\", x, y);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,37,188,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,9,NULL,NULL,NULL),(189,'2026-11-14 14:43:27.805404','MEDIUM','In a void function, \'return;\' serves as an early exit command, returning control immediately to the caller without supplying a value. Misconception: Believing void functions cannot contain any return statements or must return 0.',1,'What does the return statement do in a function whose return type is void?','MCQ',3,37,189,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,9,NULL,NULL,NULL),(190,'2026-11-14 14:43:27.808405','HARD','A static local variable is initialized once and retains its value between successive function calls across the program\'s lifetime. Calling counter() 3 times increments count from 0 to 1, 2, and 3, printing \'1 2 3 \'. Misconception: Assuming local variables always re-initialize to 0 on every call.',1,'What is printed by the following code with a static local variable?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nvoid counter() {\n    static int count = 0;\n    count++;\n    printf(\"%d \", count);\n}\nint main() {\n    counter();\n    counter();\n    counter();\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,37,190,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,9,NULL,NULL,NULL),(191,'2026-11-14 14:43:27.816404','HARD','This computes factorial: 4 * mystery(3) = 4 * 3 * mystery(2) = 4 * 3 * 2 * 1 = 24. Misconception: Confusing factorial multiplication with recursive addition (which would yield 10).',1,'What is the output of this recursive function?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint mystery(int n) {\n    if (n <= 1) return 1;\n    return n * mystery(n - 1);\n}\nint main() {\n    printf(\"%d\", mystery(4));\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',3,37,191,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,9,NULL,NULL,NULL),(192,'2026-11-14 14:43:27.819405','EASY','char holds a single 1-byte character (such as an ASCII character).',2,'Which C data type is typically used to store a single ASCII character?','MULTIPLE_CHOICE',3,32,192,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,9,NULL,NULL,NULL),(193,'2026-11-14 14:43:27.822405','EASY','Standard input/output functions printf and scanf are declared in stdio.h.',2,'What standard C header file must be included to use printf() and scanf()?','ENUMERATION',3,34,193,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,9,NULL,NULL,NULL),(194,'2026-11-14 14:43:27.823405','MEDIUM','A do-while loop checks its condition after the loop body, so it always executes at least once.',2,'How many times does a standard do-while loop execute its body at minimum?','ENUMERATION',3,36,194,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,9,NULL,NULL,NULL),(195,'2026-11-14 14:43:27.825404','HARD','Model note: switch requires integral/enum constant expressions and break to prevent fall-through; if-else supports arbitrary boolean expressions and ranges.',10,'Explain the difference between an if-else chain and a switch statement in C, including when you would choose one over the other.','ESSAY',3,35,195,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',1,9,NULL,NULL,NULL);
