INSERT INTO `resource_files` VALUES (3,'application/pdf',15412,'IT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nIT365\nDATA ANALYTICS 1\nLesson 3 : Data Cleaning, Aggregation and \nTransformation\nMattheus Marcus Contreras \nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nLearning Objectives\nAt the end of this lesson, students should be able to:\n• Explain the role of data cleaning in the analytics pipeline\n• Identify common real-world data quality issues\n• Apply Excel functions to clean, standardize, and transform \ndatasets\n• Validate cleaned data before analysis and visualization\n• Create distribution from the cleaned data\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nLesson Outline\nPart I. Foundations of Data Cleaning\nPart II. Data Cleaning Workflow\nPart III. Excel Functions for Data Cleaning, Aggregation and Transformation\nPart IV. Data Distribution\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nPart I\nFoundations of Data Cleaning\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nWhat is Data Cleaning?\nDefinition. Data Cleaning is the process of detecting incorrect, \nincomplete, inconsistent, or irrelevant data.\nData Cleaning ensures ARC:\n• Accuracy\n• Reliability\n• Consistency\nIt is the first major step in the data analytics pipeline.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nWhat is Data Transformation?\nDefinition. Data Transformation is a process under data cleaning \nwhere the data structure or representation is changed for the \npurpose of analysis and modelling. \nThis includes the following methods: splitting and merging \n(cohort), aggregating, changing data types(data transform) and \nencoding(normalization and standardization).\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nWhat is Data Aggregation?\nDefinition. Data Aggregation is a method of collecting, grouping, \nand summarizing raw data into a more useful or meaningful form.\nInstead of looking at thousands of individual records, \naggregation lets us see patterns, totals, or averages at a higher \nlevel.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nImportance of Data Cleaning\no Prevents incorrect conclusions\no Reduces errors in formulas and models\no Ensures meaningful visualizations\no Improves decision-making quality\nRule of thumb:\nPoor data quality leads to poor analytics results.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nReal-World Impact of Dirty Data\nExamples\n• Wrong sales forecasts\n• Incorrect student records\n• Misleading survey results\n• Faulty business decisions\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nCommon Data Issues\nMissing Values. These are sometimes called “Null Values”. Entries \nthat are empty,blank or in formats such as N/A, NA, \"-\", \n\"Unknown“, NaN (Python)\nDuplicate Records. Same rows with identical records. Sometimes, \ndenoted by IDs or primary keys appearing multiple times. \nIn accounting and inventory management, this are considered as \nrepeated/duplicate transactions.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nCommon Data Issues\nInconsistent Formatting. Data types or formats are not consistent \ne.g. Dates stored as text, Numbers stored as text, Mixed text \ncapitalization, etc.\nOutliers. These refer to extreme or impossible values that exist \nin the data. They can affect the data structure and overall \nbehaviour and introduce bias.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nCommon Data Issues\nIrrelevant Data. These are unnecessary columns or records \nunrelated to the research question. This may indicate an over-\ncollection of data.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nWhy Use Excel for Data Cleaning?\no Widely used in industry and \neducation.\no Built-in tools for cleaning and \ntransformation.\no Supports formulas, charts, \nPivotTables, and dashboards.\no Ideal for small to medium-sized \ndatasets. Source : Numerous AI (2023 - 2026)\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nPart II\nData Cleaning Workflow\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nThe Data \nCleaning \nWorkflow\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nThe Data Cleaning Workflow\nData Auditing\nExamine the dataset to identify errors, inconsistencies, missing \nvalues, duplicates, and other data-quality issues.\nCleaning Criteria Definition\nEstablish the rules and standards that determine what data should \nbe corrected, removed, transformed, or retained.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nThe Data Cleaning Workflow\nMethod Definition\nSelect the appropriate techniques, tools, and procedures for \naddressing the identified data-quality problems.\nVerification\nCheck whether the proposed cleaning methods and criteria are \nappropriate and will preserve the accuracy and integrity of the \ndata.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nThe Data Cleaning Workflow\nCleaning Execution\nApply the defined cleaning procedures to correct errors, handle \nmissing or inconsistent values, and remove unnecessary records.\nPost-Processing and Controlling\nReview the cleaned dataset and apply quality controls to ensure \nthat cleaning did not introduce new errors or alter valid \ninformation.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nThe Data Cleaning Workflow\nManual Cleaning\nHandle complex or ambiguous cases that automated procedures \ncannot reliably resolve and require human judgment.\nData Quality / User Quality Requirements\nDefine the expected level of data quality based on organizational \nstandards, analytical needs, and user requirements.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nThe Data Cleaning Workflow\nBackflow of Clean Data (Post-Processing)\nReturn the cleaned and validated data to the appropriate system \nor workflow so it can be reused for analysis and future \nprocessing.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nPart III\nExcel Functions for Data Cleaning, \nAggregation and Transformation\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nBasic Excel Functions for Data Cleaning\nTRIM() Function\n• Removes extra spaces from text.\n• Syntax: =TRIM(text)\nCLEAN() Function\n• Removes non-printable characters.\n• Syntax: =CLEAN(text)\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nBasic Excel Functions for Data Cleaning\nPROPER() Function\n• Capitalizes the first letter of each word.\n• Syntax: =PROPER(text)\nUPPER() and LOWER() Functions\n• All capital letters : =UPPER(text)\n• All small letters : =LOWER(text)\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nBasic Excel Functions for Data Cleaning\nSUBSTITUTE() Function\n• Replaces specific text within a string.\n• Syntax: =SUBSTITUTE(text_ref, old_text, new_text)\nREPLACE() Function\n• Replaces text based on position.\n• Syntax: =REPLACE(old_text, start_num, num_chars, new_text)\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nBasic Excel Functions for Data Cleaning\nLEFT(), RIGHT(), MID() Functions\n• Used to extract text.\n• Syntax:\n=LEFT(text, num_chars)\n=RIGHT(text, num_chars)\n=MID(text, start, num_chars)\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nBasic Excel Functions for Data Cleaning\nVALUE() Function\n• Converts numeric text to real numbers.\n• Syntax: =VALUE(text)\nDATEVALUE() Function\n• Converts text dates to date values.\n• Syntax: =DATEVALUE(text)\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nBasic Excel Functions for Data Cleaning\nIF() Function for Cleaning\n• Handles conditional replacement.\n• Syntax: =IF(logical_test, value_if_true, value_if_false)\n• Example: =IF(A2=\"\", \"Unknown\", A2)\nIFERROR() Function\n• Prevents formula errors.\n• Used when results are error (like #DIV/0!, #N/A, #VALUE!)\n• Syntax: =IFERROR(value, value_if_error)\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nBasic Excel Functions for Data Cleaning\nCONCAT() Function\n• combines different cells into one cell.\nSyntax: =CONCAT(text1, [text2], ...)\nExample:\n=CONCAT(A2,\" \",B2)\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nBasic Excel Functions for Data Aggregation\nCOUNT() Function\nPurpose: Counts the number of cells that contain numbers\nSyntax: =COUNT(value1, value2, …)\nExample: Count the number of students with recorded scores\nFormula: =COUNT(E2:E20)\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nBasic Excel Functions for Data Aggregation\nAVERAGE() Function\nPurpose: Returns the arithmetic mean of a group of numbers\nSyntax: =AVERAGE(number1, number2, …)\nExample: Calculate the average score of students\nFormula: =AVERAGE(E2:E20)\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nBasic Excel Functions for Data Aggregation\nSUM() Function\nPurpose: Returns the total of a group of numbers\nSyntax: =SUM(number1, number2, …)\nExample: Calculate the total sales\nFormula: =SUM(F2:F20)\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nBasic Excel Functions for Data Aggregation\nMAX() Function\nPurpose: Returns the largest value in a group of numbers\nSyntax: =MAX(number1, number2, …)\nExample: Find the highest score obtained by students\nFormula: =MAX(E2:E20)\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nBasic Excel Functions for Data Aggregation\nMIN() Function\nPurpose: Returns the smallest value in a group of numbers\nSyntax: =MIN(number1, number2, …)\nExample: Find the lowest score obtained by students\nFormula: =MIN(E2:E20)\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nBasic Excel Functions for Conditional and \nControl Flow\nAND() Function\nPurpose: Returns TRUE if all conditions are TRUE\nSyntax: =AND(logical1, logical2, …)\nExample: Pass only if Score ≥ 60 AND Sales ≥ 2000\nFormula:=IF(AND(E2>=60, F2>=2000), \"Pass\", \"Fail\")\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nBasic Excel Functions for Conditional and \nControl Flow\nOR() Function\nPurpose: Returns TRUE if any one condition is TRUE\nSyntax: =OR(logical1, logical2, …)\nExample: Award bonus if Sales ≥ 4000 OR Score ≥ 90\nFormula: =IF(OR(F2>=4000, E2>=90), \"Bonus\", \"No Bonus\")\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nBasic Excel Functions for Conditional and \nControl Flow\nNOT() Function\nPurpose: Reverses the result of a logical test\nSyntax: =NOT(logical)\nExample: Flag records where Region is not East\nFormula: =IF(NOT(C2=\"East\"), \"Other Region\", \"East\")\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nBasic Excel Functions for Conditional and \nControl Flow\nXOR Function (Exclusive OR)\nPurpose: Returns TRUE if only one condition is TRUE (but not \nboth)\nSyntax: =XOR(logical1, logical2, …)\nExample: Bonus if student either scored ≥ 90 or sales ≥ 4000, but \nnot both\nFormula: =IF(XOR(E2>=90, F2>=4000), \"Bonus\", \"No Bonus\")\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nBasic Excel Functions for Conditional and \nControl Flow\nIF() Function\nPurpose: Returns one value if condition is TRUE, another if FALSE\nSyntax: =IF(logical_test, value_if_true, value_if_false)\nExample: =IF(A2>=60, \"Pass\", \"Fail\")\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nBasic Excel Functions for Conditional and \nControl Flow\nIFS() Function\nPurpose: Handles multiple conditions (no nesting needed)\nSyntax: =IFS(condition1, result1, condition2, result2, …)\nExample: =IFS(A2>=90,\"A\", A2>=80,\"B\", A2>=70,\"C\", TRUE,\"F\")\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nBasic Excel Functions for Conditional and \nControl Flow\nAVERAGEIF() and SUMIF() Functions\nPurpose: Average or Sum cells based on one condition\nSyntax: \n =AVERAGEIF(range, criteria, [average_range])\n =SUMIF(range, criteria, [sum_range])\nExample: Average and Sum sales for Region = \"East“\n =AVERAGEIF(table[region], “=East”, table[sales])\n =SUMIF(table[region], “=East”, table[sales])\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nBasic Excel Functions for Conditional and \nControl Flow\nAVERAGEIFS() and SUMIFS() Functions\nPurpose: Average with multiple conditions\nSyntax: =AVERAGEIFS(average_range, criteria_range1, criteria1, …)\nExample: Average and Sum of sales where Region = \"East\" AND \nProduct = \"A“\n =AVERAGEIFS(table[sales],table[region], “=East”, table[product], “=A”)\n =SUMIFS(table[sales],table[region], “=East”, table[product], “=A”)\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nBasic Excel Functions for Conditional and \nControl Flow\nNote: There are also COUNTIF()/COUNTIFS(), MAXIF()/MAXIFS(), \nMINIF()/MINIFS(), etc. having the same syntaxes from \nAVERAGEIF()/AVERAGEIFS()\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nText-to-Columns Feature\nSplits text into multiple columns.\nUsed when you have datasets that have delimiters.\nCan be used to format dates also.\nExamples of application:\n• Full Name : First Name, Last Name\n• Date & Time separation\nShortcut Keys: Alt + A + E\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nFind & Replace Feature\nFinds specific text or values in a worksheet and replaces them \nwith new text or values\nFind: old_value -> Replace with: new_value\nExample: Replace all instances of bsit with BSIT\nShortcut Keys: Ctrl + H (or Ctrl + F then click the Replace tab)\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nPart IV\nData Distribution\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nWhy Check for Data Distribution\nWe check the distribution of data before running an analysis to \ndetermine which statistical methods are valid for the data. \nDifferent shapes of data require completely different \nmathematical tools.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nSteps in Determining the Data \nDistribution\nStep 1: Calculate the Summary Statistics\nFirst, find the basic values needed for Sturges\' Rule. \nEnter these formulas in separate cells:\nSample Size (n): =COUNT(table[data])\nMinimum Value: =MIN(table[data])\nMaximum Value: =MAX(table[data])\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nSteps in Determining the Data \nDistribution\nStep 2: Apply Sturges\' Formula for Number of Bins\nIn a new cell, calculate the ideal number of classes (bins or k):\nFormula: \n =ROUND(1 + 3.322 * LOG10(COUNT(table[data])), 0)\nNote: For 50 data points, Sturges\' rule yields approximately \n6.64. Wrapping it in =ROUND(..., 0) rounds it to 7 bins.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nSteps in Determining the Data \nDistribution\nStep 3: Calculate the Bin Width\nFind out how wide (i) each class interval should be:\nFormula: \n=ROUNDUP((MAX(table[data]) - MIN(table[data])) / Bins_Cell, 1)\nNote: Replace Bins_Cell with the cell reference from Step 2. Using ROUNDUP \nensures your bins fully cover the maximum data value. Note use 1 if data has \ndecimal values, 0 if data is integer/whole number\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nSteps in Determining the Data \nDistribution\nStep 4: Create the Bin Upper and Lower Limits\nExcel\'s histogram tool and functions require you to specify the \nupper boundary of each bin.\nIn a new column (e.g., column C), label the header \"Bin Upper \nLimit\".\nIn the first data row (C2), calculate the first upper limit: \n=MIN(table[data]) + Bin_Width_Cell\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nSteps in Determining the Data \nDistribution\nStep 4: Create the Bin Upper and Lower Limits\nIn the second row (C3), add the width to the previous bin: \n=C2 + Bin_Width_Cell\nDrag this formula down until you have created exactly the number of \nbins calculated in Step 2.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nSteps in Determining the Data \nDistribution\nStep 4: Create the Bin Upper and Lower Limits\nTo create the lower limits, just copy the previous row’s upper \nlimit. Note that the lower limit of the first entry is the minimum \nvalue. \nYou may also get the upper- and lower-class boundary by \nadding/subtracting 0.5 to the upper and lower limit.\nTo get the midpoint, just get the average of the limits.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nSteps in Determining the Data \nDistribution\nStep 5: Generate the Frequency Distribution\nYou can automatically calculate how many data points fall into each \nbin using the FREQUENCY function.\nLabel an adjacent column (e.g., column D) as \"Frequency\".\nSelect the empty cells next to your bin upper limits (e.g., D2:D8).\nType the formula: =FREQUENCY(table[data], C2:C8)\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nSteps in Determining the Data \nDistribution\nStep 5: Generate the Frequency Distribution\nCRITICAL: Do not just press Enter. Press Ctrl + Shift + Enter (or \njust Enter if you are using Excel 365) to apply it as an array \nformula.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nSteps in Determining the Data \nDistribution\nStep 6: Create the Histogram Chart\nNow that your frequency table is complete, build the visual chart:\nHighlight your Bin Upper Limits and Frequencies (cells C2:D8).\nGo to the Insert tab in the Excel ribbon.\nClick on Insert Column or Bar Chart and select Clustered Column.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nSteps in Determining the Data \nDistribution\nStep 6: Create the Histogram Chart\nRequired Formatting: \nTo make it a true histogram with no gaps between bars, right-click \nany bar on the chart, select Format Data Series, and change the Gap \nWidth to 0%.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nEND OF LESSON','Text extracted successfully.','READY','IT365 Lesson 3 - Data Cleaning, Aggregation and Transformation.pdf',57,'c5d1ad25682156fdd2d624bc57afcfc2d33115d65aebc67f0b90b12e4a161781',466304,'1352abd5-63db-4b95-9a51-72e59862f5b0.pdf','2026-11-09 12:14:31.286097',15),(4,'application/pdf',15846,'IT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nIT365\nDATA ANALYTICS 1\nLesson 4.1 : Statistical Measures (Theory)\nMattheus Marcus Contreras \nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nLearning Objectives\nAt the end of this lesson, students should be able to:\n• Explain the purpose of measures of central tendency and \nvariability\n• Differentiate between mean, median, and mode and identify \nwhen to use each\n• Explain and calculate measures of variability (range, \nvariance, standard deviation, coefficient of variation)\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nLearning Objectives\nAt the end of this lesson, students should be able to:\n• Distinguish between sample and population formulas\n• Define measures of location (fractiles).\n• Interpret boxplots and interquartile range.\n• Calculate and interpret z-scores.\n• Apply z-scores to identify outliers.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nLesson Outline\nPart I. Descriptive Analytics\nPart II. Measures of Central Tendency\nPart III. Measures of Variability\nPart IV. Measures of Shape\nPart V. Measures of Location\nPart VI. z-Score and Empirical Rule\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nPart I\nDescriptive Analytics\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nRole of Descriptive Analytics\nDescriptive Analytics helps summarize large datasets, describe \npatterns, trends, and distributions, and provides inputs for \nadvanced analytics (regression, hypothesis testing)\nFour Main Categories:\n• Measures of Central Tendency\n• Measures of Variability\n• Measures of Shape\n• Measures of Location\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nCOUNT Functions\nCOUNT Functions are used determine the number of observations in a \ndataset before analysis.\n• Determines sample size\n• Helps validate completeness of data\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nPart II\nMeasures of Central Tendency\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nWhat is Central Tendency?\nMeasures of central tendency identify the center or typical value \nof a dataset.\nRepresent data using a single value.\nEnable easy comparison across datasets.\nMeasures: Mean, Median, Mode, and Geometric Mean\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nMean (Arithmetic Mean)\nDefinition: The average of all values in a dataset. This is used \nfor metric variables. \nFormula:\nMean = σ 𝑥𝑖 = 𝑥1 + 𝑥2 + · · · + 𝑥𝑛 \n𝑛 𝑛\nExample: 30, 31, 35, 36\nMean = (30 + 31 + 35 + 36)/4 = 33\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nMedian\nThe middle value when data is arranged in ascending/descending \norder.\nRules:\n• Odd number of observations : middle value\n• Even number : average of two middle values\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nMedian\nExample: 30, 31, 35, 36, 38\nMedian = 35\nExample: 30, 31, 35, 36, 38, 40\nMedian = (35 + 36)/2 = 35.5\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nMode\nThe value that occurs most frequently.\nExample 1: 32, 42, 46, 46, 54\nMode = 46\nExample 2: 30, 32, 33, 33, 34, 34, 35\nMode = {33, 34}\nExample 2: 30, 32, 33, 34, 35, 36, 37\nMode = {} or ∅\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nGeometric Mean\nUsed to compute average growth rates over time.\nFormula: 𝟏\nGM = [ 𝒙𝟏 𝒙𝟐 ··· 𝒙𝒏 ]𝒏\nExample: 30, 31, 35, 36\nMean = [(30)(31)(35)(36)]^(1/4) ≈ 32.90\nWhy Not use Arithmetic Mean? Growth is multiplicative, not additive.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nChoosing the Right Measure\nSituation Best Measure\nNominal data Mode\nOrdinal data Median\nNo outliers Mean\nSkewed data Median\nGrowth rates Geometric Mean\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nPart III\nMeasures of Variability\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nWhat is Variability?\nMeasures of Variability describe how spread out the data values are.\nImportance of Variability:\n• Same Mean ≠ Same Consistency\n• Indicates risk, reliability, and stability\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nRange\nDifference between the maximum and minimum values.\nRange = Maximum − Minimum\nStrength: Simple\nWeakness: Ignores most data points\nExcel Functions: \n• = MAX(range)\n• = MIN(range)\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nVariance\nAverage of squared deviations from the mean.\nFormula: \nσ 𝒙𝒊−𝒎𝒆𝒂𝒏 𝟐\nPopulation Variance =\n𝑵\nσ 𝒙\nSample Variance = 𝒊−𝒎𝒆𝒂𝒏 𝟐\n𝑵−𝟏\nCharacteristics:\n• Squaring removes negative values.\n• Larger variance = greater spread.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nStandard Deviation\nSquare root of variance.\nFormula: \nσ 𝒙𝒊−𝒎𝒆𝒂𝒏 𝟐\nPopulation Variance =\n𝑵\nσ 𝒙 −\nSample Variance = 𝒊 𝒎𝒆𝒂𝒏 𝟐\n𝑵−𝟏\nCharacteristics:\n• Small SD implies values clustered near the mean.\n• Large SD implies values widely dispersed.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nCoefficient of Variation (CoV)\nRelative measure of dispersion.\nAllows comparison across datasets with different units or scales.\nFormula:\n𝒔𝒕𝒂𝒏𝒅𝒂𝒓𝒅 𝒅𝒆𝒗𝒊𝒂𝒕𝒊𝒐𝒏\nCoV = × 𝟏𝟎𝟎%\n𝒎𝒆𝒂𝒏\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nPart IV\nMeasures of Shape\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nWhy Study Distribution Shape?\nConsider two datasets with the same mean:\nDataset A: values are relatively balanced \naround the center \nDataset B: a few observations are extremely \nlarge\n \nTheir means may be similar, but their \ndistributions can look very different.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nWhy Study Distribution Shape?\nSkewness and kurtosis help identify these differences.\nWe can use them to:\n• Describe data distributions \n• Identify unusual observations \n• Assess distributional assumptions \n• Guide statistical analysis \n• Communicate characteristics of a dataset\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nSkewness\nSkewness measures the asymmetry of a probability distribution.\nA perfectly symmetric distribution has:\nSkewness tells us which direction the longer tail extends.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nSkewness\nSkewness measures the asymmetry of a probability distribution.\nA perfectly symmetric distribution has:\nSkewness tells us which direction the longer tail extends.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nSkewness\nThe skewness formula given in many textbooks is Pearson’s Second \nCoefficient of Skewness:\nSkew (g) = 3 (Mean – Median) / Standard Deviation.\nAnother common way is with Pearson’s Second Coefficient of \nSkewness:\nSkew (g) = (Mean – Mode) / Standard Deviation.\nIn Pearson’s First Coefficient, the mode (not the median) is \nused. This coefficient is specifically designed to work in cases \nwhere the mode is known or can be estimated.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nSkewness\nZero Skewness (g = 0): The distribution is perfectly symmetrical \n(like a normal distribution), where the left and right sides \nmirror each other and the mean, median, and mode are equal.\nPositive Skewness (g > 2): The distribution is right-tailed, \nmeaning it has a longer or fatter tail on the right side. The \nmean is typically greater than the median.\nNegative Skewness (g < -2): The distribution is left-tailed, \nmeaning it has a longer or fatter tail on the left side. The mean \nis typically less than the media\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nKurtosis\nKurtosis describes the behavior of a distribution\'s tails and \ncentral concentration relative to the normal distribution.\nIt is particularly useful for identifying distributions with:\n• Heavier tails\n• Lighter tails\n• Greater or lower tendency toward extreme observations\n• Important clarification\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nKurtosis\nKurtosis is often incorrectly \nexplained simply as:\n\"The peakedness of a \ndistribution.\"\nKurtosis is primarily associated \nwith tail behavior and the \npresence of extreme observations.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nKurtosis\nMesokurtic\nSimilar kurtosis to the normal distribution.\nExcess kurtosis = 0\nLeptokurtic\nHeavier tails than the normal distribution.\nExcess kurtosis > 1\nPlatykurtic\nLighter tails than the normal distribution.\nExcess kurtosis < −1\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nPart IV\nMeasures of Location\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nWhat are Measures of Location?\nMeasures of Location (or Fractiles) describe where a value lies \nrelative to other values in a dataset.\nMeasures of Location that divide an ordered dataset into equal parts.\nMain Types of Fractiles\n• Percentiles (100 parts)\n• Quartiles (4 parts)\n• Deciles (10 parts)\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nImportance of Measures of Location \nThey are useful for:\n• Comparing an individual score to a population\n• Identifying top or bottom performers\n• Detecting outliers\n• Understanding distribution spread\n• Decision-making in business analytics\nExamples: board exam rankings, income distribution, sales \nperformance, and student grades\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nPercentile\nThe p-th percentile is the value below which p% of observations fall.\nExamples:\n25th percentile : 25% of data are below this value\n90th percentile : 90% of data are below this value\nInterpretation:\nLower percentile = relatively low position\nHigher percentile = relatively high position\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nPercentile\nSteps in solving Percentile \nStep 1 : Arrange data in ascending order.\nStep 2 : Compute position: \nFor Included: 𝑘 = 𝑛 − 1 × 𝑟 + 1\nFor Excluded: 𝑘 = 𝑛 + 1 × 𝑟\nwhere n = number of observations\n r = percentile rank (in the formula, this is in decimal)\nStep 3 : Separate k into two parts:\n• Integer part = i\n• Decimal part = d\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nPercentile\nSteps in solving Percentile\nStep 4 : Determine the 𝑃𝑟 value.\nRules:\nCase 1 : If d = 0, then the percentile is the kth valued. \nCase 2 : If d > 0, then interpolate between position i and i + 1.\nFormula : 𝑷𝒓 = 𝒅 𝒙𝒊+𝟏 − 𝒙𝒊 + 𝒙𝒊\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nPercentile\nExample: To determine the 85th percentile (included) for the score \ndataset.\n20 17 8 12 11 15\n10 9 11 13 18 10\nStep 1 : Arrange the data in ascending order.\n8 9 10 10 11 11\n12 13 15 17 18 20\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nPercentile\nExample: To determine the 85th percentile for the score dataset.\nStep 2 : Compute position.\nn = 12\nr = 85th -> 0.85\n𝑘 = 𝑛 − 1 × 𝑟 + 1\n𝑘 = 12 − 1 × 0.85 + 1\n𝒌 = 𝟏𝟎. 𝟑𝟓\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nPercentile\nExample: To determine the 85th percentile for the score dataset.\nStep 3 : Separate k into two parts.\n𝑘 = 10.35\nInteger part : 𝑖 = 10\nDecimal part : 𝑑 = 0.35\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nPercentile\nExample: To determine the 85th percentile for the score dataset.\nStep 4 : Determine the Pr value.\nSince d > 0, then we will use the formula : 𝑷𝒓 = 𝒅 𝒙𝒊+𝟏 − 𝒙𝒊 + 𝒙𝒊\nNote: 𝑑 = 0.35 , 𝑥𝑖 = 𝑥10 = 17 , 𝑥10+1 = 𝑥11 = 18 \n𝑷𝟖𝟓 = (𝟎. 𝟑𝟓) 𝟏𝟖 − 𝟏𝟕 + 𝟏𝟕\n𝑷𝟖𝟓 = 𝟏𝟕. 𝟑𝟓\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nPercentile\nSteps in solving Percentile Rank (r)\nStep 1 — Arrange data in ascending order.\nStep 2 — Apply the Percentile Rank Formula:\n𝒃 + 𝟎. 𝟓𝒆\n𝒓 = × 𝟏𝟎𝟎\n𝒏\nwhere: b = number of values below the score\n e = number of values equal to the score\n n = total number of observations\nNote : Round off the answer to the nearest integer.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nPercentile\nExample: Determine the percentile rank of 11 for the score \ndataset.\n20 17 8 12 11 15\n10 9 11 13 18 10\nStep 1 : Arrange the data in ascending order.\n8 9 10 10 11 11\n12 13 15 17 18 20\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nPercentile\nExample: Determine the percentile rank of 11 for the score dataset.\nStep 2 : Apply the Percentile Rank Formula.\nPth = 11 ; n = 12 ; b = 4 ; e = 1\n𝒃 + 𝟎. 𝟓𝒆\n𝒓 = × 𝟏𝟎𝟎\n𝒏\n𝟒 + 𝟎. 𝟓(𝟐)\n𝒓 = × 𝟏𝟎𝟎\n𝟏𝟐\n𝒓 = 𝟒𝟏. 𝟔𝟕 ≈ 𝟒𝟐\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nQuartiles\nQuartiles divide data into four equal parts.\nQ1 = first quartile or 25th percentile\n𝑄2 = second quartile or 50th percentile or median\n𝑄3 = third quartile or 75th percentile\n𝑄4 = fourth quartile or 100th percentile or maximum\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nQuartiles\nInterquartile Range (IQR)\nMeasures spread of middle data. This is used to measure the spread \nto detect values considered as outliers.\n𝑰𝑸𝑹 = 𝑸𝟑 − 𝑸𝟏\nExcel Syntax\n=QUARTILE.INC(range,3) - QUARTILE.INC(range,1)\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nQuartile\nExample: Determine the IQR for the score dataset.\n20 17 8 12 11 15\n10 9 11 13 18 10\nNote : Since 𝐼𝑄𝑅 = 𝑄3 – 𝑄1, We need to solve for 𝑄1 = 𝑃25 and 𝑄3 = 𝑃75.\nBut first, let’s arrange the data in ascending order.\n8 9 10 10 11 11\n12 13 15 17 18 20\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nPercentile\nFor 𝑸𝟏 : n = 12, r = 0.25\n𝑘 = 𝑛 − 1 × 𝑟 + 1 = 12 − 1 0.25 + 1 = 𝟑. 𝟕𝟓\n𝑖 = 3 and 𝑑 = 0.75\n𝑥𝑖 = 𝑥3 = 10 , 𝑥3+1 = 𝑥4 = 10 \n𝑃𝑟 = 𝑑 𝑥𝑖+1 − 𝑥𝑖 + 𝑥𝑖\n𝑃25 = 0.75 10 − 10 + 10\n𝑷𝟐𝟓 = 𝑸𝟏 = 𝟏𝟎\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nPercentile\nFor 𝑸𝟑 : n = 12, r = 0.75\n𝑘 = 𝑛 − 1 × 𝑟 + 1 = 12 − 1 0.75 + 1 = 𝟗. 𝟐𝟓\n𝑖 = 9 and 𝑑 = 0.25\n𝑥𝑖 = 𝑥9 = 15 , 𝑥9+1 = 𝑥10 = 17 \n𝑃𝑟 = 𝑑 𝑥𝑖+1 − 𝑥𝑖 + 𝑥𝑖\n𝑃75 = 0.25 17 − 15 + 15\n𝑷𝟕𝟓 = 𝑸𝟑 = 𝟏𝟓. 𝟓\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nPercentile\nFor 𝑰𝑸𝑹: Q1 = 10, Q3 = 15. 5\n𝐼𝑄𝑅 = 𝑄3 − 𝑄1\n𝐼𝑄𝑅 = 15.5 − 10\n𝑰𝑸𝑹 = 𝟓. 𝟓\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nDeciles\nDeciles divide data into 10 equal parts.\n𝐷1 = 10th percentile 𝐷7 = 70th percentile\n𝐷2 = 20th percentile 𝐷8 = 80th percentile\n𝐷3 = 30th percentile 𝐷9 = 90th percentile\n𝐷4 = 20th percentile 𝐷10 = 100th percentile = maximum\n𝐷5 = 50th percentile or median or 2nd quartile\n𝐷6 = 60th percentile\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nDeciles\nExample: Determine the 3rd decile (Excluded) for the score dataset.\n20 17 8 12 11 15\n10 9 11 13 18 10\nArrange the data in ascending order.\n8 9 10 10 11 11\n12 13 15 17 18 20\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nDeciles\nFor 𝑫𝟑 : n = 12, r = 0.30 (note: use excluded formula)\n𝒌 = 𝒏 + 𝟏 × 𝒓 = 12 + 1 0.30 = 𝟑. 𝟗\n𝑖 = 3 and 𝑑 = 0.9\n𝑥𝑖 = 𝑥3 = 10 , 𝑥3+1 = 𝑥4 = 10 \n𝑃𝑟 = 𝑑 𝑥𝑖+1 − 𝑥𝑖 + 𝑥𝑖\n𝑃30 = 0.9 10 − 10 + 10\n𝑷𝟑𝟎 = 𝑫𝟑 = 𝟏𝟎\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nPart V\nz-Score and Empirical Rule\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nWhat is a z-Score\nA z-score measures how far a value is from the mean in terms of \nstandard deviations. This standardizes data for comparison.\nFormula:\n𝒙 − 𝝁\n𝒛 =\n𝜹\nwhere:\n𝑥 = data value\n𝜇 = mean\n𝛿 = standard deviation\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nZ-Score Interpretation\nZ-Score Meaning\n0 Exactly average\nPositive Above mean\nNegative Below mean\n<+2 Very high\n>−2 Very low\n<=-3 Negative outlier\n>=+3 Positive outlier\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nImportance of z-Scores\n• Comparing datasets with different scales\n• Identifying unusual values\n• Risk analysis\n• Performance comparison\n• Machine learning preprocessing\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nExample of z-Score Computation\nGiven the following data:\n1 2 17 18 19 20 30\nSupposed that the sample standard deviation is 4.4. Find which \ndata is/are outliers.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nExample of z-Score Computation\nSolve first for the mean.\n1+2+17+18+19+20+30 \n𝜇 = ≈ 15.29\n7\nThen Create a table with data and z-score column.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nExample of z-Score Computation\nData z-Score\n1 − 15.29\n1 ≈ −3.25\n4.4\n2 − 15.29\n≈ −3.02\n4.4\n2 Since 1, 2 and 30 have z-scores \n17 − 15.29\n17 ≈ 0.39\n4.4 beyond 3, then they are considered \n18 − 15.29\n18 ≈ 0.62\n4.4 as outliers.\n19 − 15.29\n19 ≈ 0.84\n4.4\n20 − 15.29\n20 ≈ 1.07\n4.4\n30 − 15.29\n30 ≈ 3.34\n4.4\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nEmpirical Rule\nFor bell-shaped distributions:\n68.3% within 1 SD\n95.4% within 2 SD\n99.7% within 3 SD\nThis helps estimate probability ranges.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nEmpirical Rule\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nIQR Method\nThe Interquartile Range (IQR) Method identifies potential outliers \nbased on the middle 50% of the data.\nThen calculate the boundaries:\nLower Bound = 𝑄1 − 1.5 𝐼𝑄𝑅\nUpper Bound = 𝑄3 + 1.5 𝐼𝑄𝑅\nValues:\nBelow the lower bound -> potential outlier \nAbove the upper bound -> potential outlier\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nz-score method\nA general rule is: ∣ 𝑧 ∣> 3 is a potential outlier\nFor small sample size (n < 30 ) use ∣ 𝑧 ∣> 2.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nSeatwork (1 whole yellow paper – B2B) Data\nGiven the following data set. Show complete solution. 10\n17\n(1) Solve for the Mean, Median and Mode. 15\n(2) Compute for the Variance and Standard Deviation. 28\n(3) Solve for the Skewness. Determine the type of skewness. 13\n13\n(4) Determine 𝑄1, 𝑄3 and IQR. 12\n(5) What Percentile Rank does the data 17 belong to. 16\n(6) Create a table to determine z-scores of all data entries. 18\n(7) Solve for the boundaries using IQR Method. Which data is/are 14\nconsidered as outlier/s using IQR Method?\n(8) Refer to the z-scores at (6). Which data is/are considered as \noutlier/s using z-score Method?\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nEND OF LESSON','Text extracted successfully.','READY','IT365 Lesson 4.1 - Statistical Measures (Theory).pdf',68,'9312339089d80f70088e0cf9429eb9479978d7afa8dbe90a4c4ab8b429491bbc',589027,'80a54895-defd-466e-ae38-44c7f15e890c.pdf','2026-11-09 13:00:57.176773',28),(5,'application/pdf',11448,'IT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nIT365\nDATA ANALYTICS 1\nLesson 1 : Introduction to Data Analytics\nMattheus Marcus Contreras \nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nLearning Objectives\nAt the end of this lesson, students should be able to:\n• Define data analytics and identify its analytical methods\n• Explain the data analytics process from data collection to \ndecision-making\n• Understand core Big Data concepts and technologies\n• Identify real-world applications of business analytics across \nindustries\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nLesson Outline\nPart I. Data Analytics and Analytical Methods\nPart II. Data Analytics Process\nPart III. Big Data\nPart IV. Applications of Data/Business Analytics\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nPart I\nData Analytics and Analytical Methods\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nWhat is Data Analytics?\nData Analytics process of examining raw data to uncover patterns, \ntrends, relationships, and useful insights that can support \ndecision-making.\nIt involves collecting, cleaning, transforming, and analyzing \ndata using statistical, mathematical, and computational \ntechniques to identify meaningful information and guide actions.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nData Analytics vs Data Analysis\nData Analysis focuses on examining and interpreting data to \nanswer specific questions.\nData Analytics is a broader discipline that includes data \nanalysis plus the entire process of turning data into decisions.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nImportance of Data Analytics\nData analytics helps organizations to:\n• Make evidence-based decisions\n• Improve efficiency and productivity\n• Reduce risks and uncertainty\n• Gain competitive advantage\n• Predict future outcomes\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nData-driven/centered \nCareers\nData Science, Data Analytics, and \nData Engineering are careers that \nhave become increasingly \nimportant in today’s data-driven \nworld.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nDevelopment of Data Analytics\nEarly Data Processing Era (1960s–1980s)\nManual and basic computer-based data handling.\nCharacteristics:\n• Data stored in physical files or early databases (hierarchical, flat files).\n• Limited analytical power — mostly reporting and simple statistics.\n• Batch processing, mainframe computers.\nTechnologies: COBOL, punch cards, early SQL databases.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nDevelopment of Data Analytics\nBusiness Intelligence Era (1990s–2000s)\nSystematic reporting and dashboard creation for business decisions.\nCharacteristics:\n• Emergence of relational databases and data warehouses.\n• ETL (Extract, Transform, Load) processes for structured data.\n• Use of OLAP (Online Analytical Processing) for multi-dimensional analysis.\nTechnologies: Oracle, Microsoft SQL Server, SAP BusinessObjects.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nDevelopment of Data Analytics\nBig Data & Advanced Analytics Era (2010s–Present)\nReal-time, large-scale analytics powered by AI and machine learning.\nCharacteristics:\n• Explosion of unstructured/semi-structured data from IoT, social media, \nsensors.\n• Use of distributed computing (Hadoop, Spark).\n• Cloud-based analytics platforms and real-time streaming data analysis.\nTechnologies: Hadoop, Spark, AWS, Azure ML, TensorFlow.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nDevelopment of Data Analytics\nGoogle Trends Graph of Searches on the term Data Analytics (2004 – present)\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nCategorization of Analytical Methods\n(Types of Data Analytics)\nDescriptive Analytics\n• Answers “What happened?”\n• Summarizes data to identify patterns, trends, and anomalies.\n• Methods & Tools\no Data Aggregation and Data Mining\no Summary Statistics, Dashboards, and Reports\nExample: Monthly sales report showing revenue trends.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nCategorization of Analytical Methods\n(Types of Data Analytics)\nDiagnostic Analytics\n• Answers “Why did it happen?”\n• Explores historical data to find causes and contributing factors \nof events.\n• Methods & Tools\no Root Cause Analysis\no Drill-Down and Data Discovery Techniques\nExample: Identifying reasons for a sudden drop in website traffic.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1 COURSE ORIENTATION\nCategorization of Analytical Methods\n(Types of Data Analytics)\nPredictive Analytics\n• Answers “What is likely to happen?”\n• Uses statistical models and machine learning to forecast future \noutcomes.\n• Methods & Tools\no Regression Analysis\no Time Series Forecasting\nExample: Predicting next quarter’s sales based on historical trends.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nCategorization of Analytical Methods\n(Types of Data Analytics)\nPrescriptive Analytics\n• Answers “What should we do?”\n• Recommends optimal actions to achieve desired results.\n• Methods & Tools\no Optimization Algorithms\no Simulation Modeling\nExample: Suggesting the best marketing strategy to maximize customer \nengagement.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nCommon Analytical Methods\n• Statistical Analysis\n• Regression Analysis\n• Classification\n• Clustering\n• Time-series Analysis\n• Machine Learning Algorithms\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nPart II\nData Analytics Process\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nProblem Definition\nData Collection\nData Analytics \nProcess Data Cleaning\nData Exploration\nA structured approach to \nanalytics ensures accuracy and Data Analysis & Modeling\nusefulness of results.\nInterpretation & Visualization\nDecision-Making\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nStep 1: Problem Definition\n• Clearly define the business or research question.\n• Identify objectives and success metrics.\n• This must be SMART (Specific, Measurable, Attainable, Result-\nOriented, Time-bounded)\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nStep 1: Problem Definition\nExample:\nPoor Problem Statement: \n“How can we reduce student drop-out rates?” \nGood Problem Statement: \n“How can we reduce the student drop-out rate by at least 10% \namong second-year college students within one academic year \nthrough targeted academic support and early intervention \nprograms?”\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nStep 2: Data Collection\n• In this step, data accuracy is critical.\n• Sources of data:\no Databases\no Surveys and Questionnaires\no Sensors and IoT Devices\no Web and Social Media\no Transaction Records\nExample: Collecting attendance and grade records.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nStep 2: Data Collection\nGarbage In, Garbage Out (GIGO) – poor data leads to poor \ndecisions\nGarbage \nIn\nProcess\nGarbage \nOut\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nStep 3: Data Cleaning\nSince poor data quality can lead to misleading results, in this \nstep, we can mitigate by:\n• Handling Missing Values\n• Removing Duplicates\n• Correcting Errors and Inconsistencies\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nStep 4: Data Exploration\nExploratory Data Analysis (EDA) is the initial process of \nexamining data to identify patterns, detect anomalies(outliers), \ntest assumptions, and summarize key features using \nvisualizations and basic statistics before formal modeling.\nTools:\n• Summary Statistics\n• Charts and Graphs\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nStep 5: Data Analysis & Modeling\nIn this step, we apply analytical techniques and build \nstatistical or machine learning models.\nExample:\nUsing regression to analyze the effect of study hours on grades.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nStep 6: Interpretation & Visualization\n• Translate results into meaningful insights\n• Use dashboards, charts, and reports\nExample:\nVisualizing sales trends using line graphs.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nStep 7: Decision-Making\nInsights are used to support strategic, tactical or operational \ndecisions.\nExample: Adjusting teaching strategies based on performance \ndata.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nStep 7: Decision-Making\nStrategic Decisions \n• Involve higher-level issues concerned with the overall \ndirection of the organization.\n• These decisions define the organization’s overall goals and \naspirations for the future.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nStep 7: Decision-Making\nTactical Decisions\n• Concern how the organization should achieve the goals and \nobjectives set by its strategy.\n• They are usually the responsibility of midlevel management.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nStep 7: Decision-Making\nOperational Decisions\n• Affect how the firm is run from day to day.\n• They are the domain of operations managers, who are the \nclosest to the customer.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nPart III\nBig Data\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nWhat is Big Data?\nBig Data refers to datasets whose size, complexity, and rate of \ngrowth exceed the capability of traditional data management \ntools to capture, store, process, and analyze effectively.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nTraditional Data vs Big Data\nTraditional Data\n• Typically, structured and stored in relational databases.\n• Collected from limited, known sources.\n• Manageable with standard tools like SQL, Excel.\n• Suitable for smaller datasets with predictable growth.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nTraditional Data vs Big Data\nBig Data\n• Extremely large, complex datasets.\n• Often unstructured or semi-structured.\n• Requires advanced tools like Hadoop, Spark, NoSQL databases.\n• Generated from diverse sources: social media, IoT devices, \nsensors.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nCharacteristics of Big Data (5 Vs)\nVolume – Massive amounts of \ndata.\nVelocity – Rapid data \ngeneration and processing.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nCharacteristics of Big Data (5 Vs)\nVariety – Different formats: text, \nimages, video, sensor data.\nVeracity – Data accuracy and \nreliability.\nValue – Insights and benefits derived \nfrom analysis.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nPart IV\nApplications of Data/Business Analytics\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nWhat is Business Analytics?\nBusiness Analytics uses data, statistical analysis, and \npredictive models to support business decision-making.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nApplications of Business Analytics\nCustomer Segmentation and Targeting\n• Identify distinct groups of customers based on behavior, \npreferences, and demographics.\n• Helps in designing personalized marketing strategies to \nimprove engagement and sales.\nExample: An e-commerce store grouping customers into “bargain \nhunters” and “premium buyers.”\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nApplications of Business Analytics\nDemand Forecasting and Inventory Optimization\n• Predict future product demand to ensure optimal stock levels.\n• Reduces overstock and stockouts, improving operational \nefficiency.\nExample: A retail chain forecasting seasonal demand for holiday \nproducts.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nApplications of Business Analytics\nFraud Detection and Risk Management\n• Detect suspicious transactions and prevent financial losses.\n• Protects businesses from fraud, theft, and data breaches.\nExample: A bank using analytics to detect unusual credit card \nspending patterns.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nApplications of Business Analytics\nOperational Efficiency Improvement\n• Analyze internal processes to identify bottlenecks and \ninefficiencies.\n• Lowers costs and speeds up operations.\nExample: A manufacturing plant optimizing production scheduling \nto reduce downtime.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nApplications of Business Analytics\nMarketing Campaign Performance Analysis\n• Evaluate the effectiveness of marketing campaigns across \nchannels.\n• Helps allocate budget to the most successful campaigns.\nExample: Measuring ROI of Facebook ads versus email marketing \ncampaigns.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nApplications of Business Analytics\nProduct and Service Development\n• Use analytics to identify customer needs and improve offerings.\n• Increases customer satisfaction and competitiveness.\nExample: Streaming platforms analyzing viewing data to decide what \ncontent to produce next.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nEND OF LESSON','Text extracted successfully.','READY','IT365 Lesson 1 - Introduction to Data Analytics.pdf',47,'856c6fe3878601c9084b2c852596341c8de1021e385d0d4e998c677f2bb570b3',362567,'0aa71d53-ddff-4c0a-9df6-a8a41de3ef39.pdf','2026-11-10 06:58:02.511802',29),(6,'application/pdf',15508,'IT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nIT365\nDATA ANALYTICS 1\nLesson 2 : Overview of Data\nMattheus Marcus Contreras \nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nLearning Objectives\nAt the end of this lesson, students should be able to:\n• Define data and explain its role in data analytics\n• Distinguish different types of data and data sources\n• Differentiate qualitative and quantitative data\n• Explain population, samples, and levels of measurement\n• Understand core principles of data quality\n• Describe fundamental data preparation and preprocessing \ntechniques\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nLesson Outline\nPart I. Foundations of Data\nPart II. Types of Data and Data Sources\nPart III. Sampling and Measurement\nPart IV. Data Quality and Data Preparation\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nPart I\nFoundations of Data\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nWhat is Data?\nData refers to raw, unprocessed facts, figures, symbols, or \nobservations that can be recorded, stored, and analyzed.\nBy itself, data may not carry meaning until it is organized and \ninterpreted.\nFacts: Room Temperature of 23.5°C\nFigures: Sales Numbers, Exam Scores\nObservations: Customer Purchase Behavior\nSymbols: Text, Images, Codes\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nKey Characteristics of Data\n• Data is inherently raw and unstructured prior to processing\n• It may exist in multiple formats: numerical, textual, visual, audio, or \nsensor-based\n• It serves as the foundation for information, analysis, and decision-making\n• The value of data increases through organization, cleaning, and \ninterpretation\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nCore Concepts of Data\nVariable\nA measurable characteristic that can assume different values.\nObservation\nA set of values recorded across variables.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nCore Concepts of Data\nVariation\nThis refers to the differences among observations.\nRandom (Uncertain) Variable \nA variable whose value cannot be predicted with certainty.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nData, Information, and Knowledge\nDATA → processed into → INFORMATION → interpreted into → KNOWLEDGE\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nCore Concepts of Data\nData - Raw, unprocessed facts, figures, or observations that \nhave not yet been organized or interpreted.\nInformation - Data that has been processed, structured, and \nsummarized to provide context, relevance, and meaning.\nKnowledge - Insights derived from analyzing and interpreting \ninformation, enabling informed decision-making, problem-solving, \nand strategic action.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nCore Concepts of Data\nExample\nData: Sales Record of 120, 130, 125, 140\nInformation: Average weekly sales = 128 units\nKnowledge: Sales are stable; pricing strategy may remain \nunchanged.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nPart II\nTypes of Data and Data Sources\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nTypes of Data by Structure\nStructured Data\no Organized in rows and columns (tables)\no Stored in relational databases\no Easy to query and analyze\no Most used in organizations\nExamples: Sales Records, Customer Tables\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nTypes of Data by Structure\nSemi-Structured Data\no Does not follow a strict tabular format (semi-tabular)\no Contains some level of organization(tags, keys, or metadata)\no Commonly stored in hierarchical or nested formats\no Often used in web applications, system logs, and APIs\nExamples:\nJSON (JavaScript Object Notation), XML (Extensible Markup \nLanguage), Log files, Email headers, NoSQL database documents\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nTypes of Data by Structure\nUnstructured Data\no No predefined structure or schema\no Composed of free-form content (more complex to analyze)\no Requires specialized tools and techniques\no Represents a large proportion of real-world data\no Used in sentiment, content, and multimedia analytics\nExamples: Emails, Social media posts, Images, Audio Recordings, \nVideos, Website Content and Multimedia Files\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nTypes of Data by Nature\nQualitative Data\no Descriptive and categorical (labels)\no Represents characteristics or attributes\nExamples: Customer Reviews, Colors, Job Titles\nQuantitative Data\no Numerical and measurable\no Used in statistical analysis\nExamples: Age, Income, Temperature\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nTypes of Data Sources\nPrimary Data Sources\no Data that is collected firsthand by the researcher or \norganization for a specific purpose\no Tailored to address a particular research question or \nanalytical objective\no Offers greater control over data quality, relevance, and \naccuracy\no Common in academic research, business analytics, and market \nstudies\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nTypes of Data Sources\nPrimary Data Sources\no more current and context-specific, but may require more time \nand resources to collect\nExamples:\nSurveys, Questionnaires, Controlled Experiments, Interviews, \nFocus Group Discussions, Direct Observations, field studies, \nSensor and IoT Data\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nTypes of Data Sources\nSecondary Data Sources\no Data that has been previously collected, processed, and \npublished by other individuals or organizations\no Used for background research, benchmarking, trend analysis, \nor exploratory studies\no Cost-effective and time-efficient, but less specific to the \ncurrent research problem\no Quality depends on the credibility, methodology, and update \nfrequency of the original source\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nTypes of Data Sources\nSecondary Data Sources\nExamples:\no Government and public databases\no Academic journals and published research reports\no Industry and market analysis reports\no Corporate records and financial statements\no Open data repositories (World Bank, Kaggle, national \nstatistics offices)\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nInternal Data vs External Data\nInternal Data\no Generated within an organization\no Sales records, employee metrics\nExternal Data\no Obtained from outside sources\no Market data, public datasets, social media\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nDigital and Emerging Data Sources\no IoT sensor data\no Social media and web analytics\no Mobile application usage data\no Open government datasets\no Kaggle and research repositories\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nPart III\nSampling and Measurement\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nPopulation and Sample\nPopulation\no The entire group of individuals, objects, or events that is \nthe focus of a study\no Represents the full scope of elements about which conclusions \nare intended to be drawn\no May include people, organizations, transactions, \nmeasurements, or observations\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nPopulation and Sample\nSample\no A subset of the population selected for detailed analysis\no Intended to represent the characteristics of the larger \npopulation\no Used when studying the entire population is impractical, \ncostly, or time-consuming\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nPopulation and Sample\nImportance of Sampling\no Reduces data collection cost, time, and effort\no Enables statistical inference, allowing conclusions about the \npopulation based on sample data\no Facilitates efficient and scalable analysis in real-world \nresearch and business contexts\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nSampling Formulas\nSlovin-Yamane Formula\nSlovin\'s formula is a simple formula used to determine the sample \nsize when the total population is known but there is limited \ninformation about the population\'s characteristics.\n𝑁\n𝑛 =\n1 + 𝑁𝑒2\nWhere:\n• n = required sample size \n• N = total population size \n• e = margin of error or level of precision, expressed as a decimal\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nSampling Formulas\nSlovin-Yamane Formula\nCommonly used when:\n• The total population size is known. \n• The population is finite. \n• The researcher wants to determine a sample based on a \nspecified margin of error. \n• There is limited information about population variance or \nproportions. \n• The study involves a basic survey or descriptive research.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nSampling Formulas\nCochran Formula\nCochran\'s formula is used to determine the sample size needed to \nestimate a population proportion when the population is large or \nits size is initially treated as infinite.\n𝑍2𝑝𝑞\n𝑛 =\n𝑒2\nWhere:\nn = initial required sample size \nZ = Z-score corresponding to the desired confidence level \np = estimated proportion of the population with the characteristic of interest \nq = 1−p \ne = desired margin of error\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nSampling Formulas\nCochran Formula\nCommon z-values: Confidence Level (Z)\n90% 1.645\n95% 1.96\n99% 2.576\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nSampling Formulas\nCochran Formula\nCochran\'s formula is appropriate when:\n• You are estimating a population proportion or percentage. \n• You know the desired confidence level. \n• You know the desired margin of error. \n• The population is very large, or you initially treat it as infinite. \n• You have an estimate of the expected proportion p, or you use p=0.50 \nwhen no reliable estimate is available.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nQualitative Data and Quantitative Data\nQualitative Data\no Non-numeric data that describes qualities, attributes, or \ncharacteristics\no Focuses on meaning, categories, themes, and interpretations \no Useful for understanding opinions, behaviors, experiences, \nand social phenomena\no Commonly analyzed using thematic analysis, content analysis, \nor coding techniques\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nQualitative Data and Quantitative Data\nQuantitative Data\no Numeric data that represents measurable quantities or counts\no Focuses on measurement, comparison, and statistical \nrelationships\no Suitable for mathematical operations, statistical testing, \nand predictive modeling\no Enables objective analysis through descriptive and \ninferential statistics\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nQualitative Data and Quantitative Data\nTYPES OF DATA\nQuantitative Data Qualitative Data\n• Investments • The weather\n• Interest rates • State and federal \n• Inventory levels legislation\n• Demand • Technological breakthroughs\n• Labor cost\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nLevels of Measurement\nLevels of measurement determine how data can be analyzed and \nwhich statistical methods apply.\nFour Levels:\no Nominal\no Ordinal\no Interval\no Ratio\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nLevels of Measurement\nNominal Level\no Categories with no inherent order\no Names, assignments and labels\nExamples:\n• Gender\n• Blood Type\n• Cuisine Type\n• Phone Number\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nLevels of Measurement\nOrdinal Level\no Ordered/ranked categories\no Tells the position but not the differences/distances between \nranks\nExamples:\n• Satisfaction Ratings\n• Education Levels\n• Competition Rankings\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nLevels of Measurement\nInterval Level\no Numeric with equal intervals\no No true/meaningful zero\nExamples:\n• Temperature (°C, °F)\n• IQ scores\n• Calendar years\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nLevels of Measurement\nRatio Level\no Numeric with equal intervals\no Has a true zero; allows meaningful ratios\nExamples:\n• Height\n• Age\n• Income\n• Distance\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nLevels of Measurement\nRatio Highest Level\nQuantitative Metric\nInterval\nDATA\nOrdinal\nQualitative Categorical\nNominal\nLowest Level\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nCommon Statistical Methods by Level\nNominal: Mode, Chi-square\nOrdinal: Median, Spearman Correlation\nInterval/Ratio: Mean, Pearson correlation, t-test, ANOVA\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nPart IV\nData Quality and Data Preparation\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nImportance of Data Quality\nHigh-quality data is essential for:\n• Accurate analysis\n• Reliable decision-making\n• Ethical and responsible analytics\n• Avoiding misleading conclusions\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nKey Dimensions of Data Quality\nConsistency — No contradictions across datasets\nUniqueness — No duplicate records\nTimeliness — Up-to-date and relevant\nValidity — Conforms to expected rules and formats\nAccuracy — Data correctly represents reality\nCompleteness — No missing critical values\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nCommon Data Quality Issues\n• Missing Values (#NULL! or #N/A)\n• Duplicate Records\n• Measurement Errors (#DIV/0!, #REF!, #NUM!)\n• Outliers and Anomalies (#VALUE!, #NAME?)\n• Inconsistent Formats\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nData Preparation\nData Preparation is the process of transforming raw data into a \nclean, structured, and analysis-ready format.\nIt is one of the most time-consuming stages in data analytics.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nCore Concepts of Data Preparation \n• Data Cleaning\n• Missing Values Handling\n• Removing Duplicates\n• Outlier Detection\n• Data Transformation\n• Feature Scaling and Normalization\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nData Cleaning\no The process of identifying and correcting errors, \ninconsistencies, and inaccuracies in datasets\no Ensures that data is accurate, reliable, and suitable for \nanalysis\no Improves the validity and credibility of analytical results\no Common tasks include correcting data entry errors, \nstandardizing formats, and removing invalid records\no Considered a critical step in the data analytics workflow\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nMissing/Null Values Handling\no Missing data occurs when values are absent or not recorded in a dataset\no If unmanaged, missing values can bias results and reduce model accuracy\no Common strategies include:\n• Removing incomplete records\n• Replacing values using mean, median, or mode\n• Predicting values using statistical or machine learning models\no The appropriate method depends on the amount, pattern, and importance \nof missing data\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nRemoving Duplicates\no Duplicate records occur when the same data entry appears more than \nonce\no Can lead to overrepresentation, biased analysis, and incorrect \ninsights\no Requires identifying records with matching identifiers or identical \nattributes\no Ensures that each observation in the dataset represents a unique \nreal-world entity\no Essential for maintaining data integrity and analytical accuracy\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nOutlier Detection\no Outliers are extreme or unusual values that differ significantly from \nmost data points\no Outliers should be investigated before removal to avoid losing valuable \ninsights\no Detecting outliers helps improve model robustness and analytical \nreliability\no Common detection methods include:\n• Z-score analysis\n• Interquartile Range (IQR)\n• Data visualization (ex. boxplots, scatterplots)\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nData Transformation\no The process of converting data into a format suitable for analysis or \nmodeling\no Improves compatibility with statistical and machine learning algorithms\no Common transformation techniques include:\n• Encoding categorical variables (e.g., label encoding, one-hot \nencoding)\n• Aggregating or grouping data\n• Creating new features through feature engineering\no Helps improve interpretability and predictive performance\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nFeature Scaling and Normalization\no Techniques used to standardize numeric variables so they are on a \ncomparable scale\no Prevents features with large numeric ranges from dominating analytical \nmodels\no Improves the performance of distance-based and gradient-based \nalgorithms\no Common Methods:\n• Normalization (Min–Max Scaling): Rescales values to a fixed range\n• Standardization (Z-score Scaling): Centers values around the mean \nwith unit variance\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nFeature Scaling and Normalization\no Techniques used to standardize numeric variables so they are on a \ncomparable scale\no Prevents features with large numeric ranges from dominating analytical \nmodels\no Improves the performance of distance-based and gradient-based \nalgorithms\no Common Methods:\n• Normalization (Min–Max Scaling): Rescales values to a fixed range\n• Standardization (Z-score Scaling): Centers values around the mean \nwith unit variance\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nEND OF LESSON','Text extracted successfully.','READY','IT365 Lesson 2 - Overview of Data.pdf',55,'d36ce8af9fabdf1b2c5a7ac9f25ce06fbead9cf89d8971ca07cbf0239dd32b1b',328114,'6ffc1114-f544-4268-a5a7-e308e1cc390a.pdf','2026-11-10 07:20:47.520336',30),(7,'application/pdf',5072,'IT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nIT365\nDATA ANALYTICS 1\nLesson 5.2 : \nVisualization Part 2\nMattheus Marcus Contreras \nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nLearning Objectives\nAt the end of this lesson, students should be able to:\n• Create and customize advanced charts such as Radar, \nWaterfall, Hierarchy, Map, and Stock charts.\n• Explain what Power BI is and identify its three main \ncomponents\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nLearning Objectives\nAt the end of this lesson, students should be able to:\n• Import and clean data using Power Query\n• Build a basic star-schema data model\n• Create interactive visuals and dashboards\n• Write basic DAX measures and calculated columns\n• Use CALCULATE and time intelligence functions\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nLesson Outline\nPart I. Specialized Charts\nPart II. Introduction to PowerBI\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nPart II\nSpecialized Charts\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nBox Plot \nor Box-and-\nWhisker Plot\nDisplay the distribution of a \ndataset using quartiles. It \nsummarizes data through the \nminimum, first quartile (Q1), \nmedian, third quartile (Q3), and \nmaximum values, making it useful \nfor understanding spread, central \ntendency, and outliers.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nBox Plot \nor Box-and-\nWhisker Plot\nWhen to Use a Box Plot\n• Comparing data distributions \nbetween groups.\n• Identifying outliers in a \ndataset.\n• Understanding data spread and \nvariability.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nBox Plot \nor Box-and-\nWhisker Plot\nExample Scenario\nA teacher compares the \ndistribution of exam scores from \ndifferent classes\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nRadar/Spider Chart \nDisplays multivariate data across \nmultiple dimensions.\nData points are connected in a \ncircular, spider-like web.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nRadar/Spider Chart \nWhen to use:\n• Comparing performance across \ncategories.\n• Showing strengths and weaknesses \n(skills assessment, product \nfeatures).\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nRadar/Spider Chart \nExample:\nComparing 3 employees’ skills \n(communication, technical, \nteamwork, creativity)\nComparing students’ scores to \ndetermine their strong and weak \nsubjects\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nRadar/Spider Chart \nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nMap Chart\nVisualizes data on a geographic map (country, region, city).\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nMap Chart\nWhen to use:\nShowing \nworld/national/regional \npopulation distribution \ndata.\nAny dataset tied to \ngeography.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nMap Chart\nExample:\nSales performance by \nregion (Luzon, Visayas, \nMindanao)\nWorld Population data.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nDoughnut Chart\nSimilar to a pie chart, but with \na hole in the center.\nCan handle multiple data series.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nDoughnut Chart\nWhen to use:\nShowing proportions of a whole \nbut with sub-proportions.\nDisplaying percentage breakdowns \nwith more style than pie.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nDoughnut Chart\nExample:\nMarket share of different brands \nwith their respective products.\nPopulation of a school per year \nlevel and gender.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nWaterfall Chart\nUsed to visualize how an initial value changes through a series of \npositive and negative contributions, leading to a final result. \nIt helps show the step-by-step impact of increases and decreases in \na dataset.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nWaterfall Chart\nExample Scenarios:\nA project manager shows how equipment costs, labor, and additional \nfunding change the total project budget.\nA business analyzes how starting revenue increases with product \nsales but decreases due to operational expenses.\nA startup presents how initial investment, operating costs, and \nadditional income streams lead to the final balance.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nStock Chart\nSpecialized chart for financial \ndata.\nShows stock price movement: Open, \nHigh, Low, Close (OHLC).\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nStock Chart\nWhen to use:\nFinancial or stock market data \nanalysis.\nTracking fluctuations in prices \nover time.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nStock Chart\nExample:\nDaily price movement of a stock \nfor one month.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nTreemap/Hierarchy \nChart\nDisplays hierarchical data \nas nested rectangles.\nEach rectangle size\nrepresents a value; color\noften shows category.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nTreemap/Hierarchy \nChart\nWhen to use:\nComparing parts of a whole in \nhierarchical datasets.\nHighlighting which categories \ncontribute most.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nTreemap/Hierarchy \nChart\nExample:\nSales by product categories and \nsubcategories.\nComparing traffic from channels \n(Social Media, Organic Search, \nPaid Ads) and their sub-sources \n(Facebook, Instagram, Google \nAds).\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nBubble Chart\nA variation of a scatter plot \nthat displays three variables in \na single chart.\nComparing three different data \nvariables simultaneously.\nVisualizing relationships between \ndatasets with different sizes or \nmagnitudes.\nIdentifying patterns, clusters, \nor outliers in complex data.\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nBubble Chart\nExample:\nA marketing analyst compares \nadvertising budget, sales \nrevenue, and number of customers \nfor different product campaigns\nIT365 - DATA ANALYTICS 1\nEND OF LESSON','Text extracted successfully.','READY','IT365 Lesson 5.2 - Visualization Part 2 v2.pdf',29,'ed8cac30f5ff7d73a8233dd92708b3ac1bc49ca252e70c87381ad87cf0a1fbc0',526220,'5315f053-eef9-43e1-bf60-958ae9aa2fa7.pdf','2026-11-10 07:44:25.757598',31),(8,'application/pdf',12671,'CSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nCSIT238\nHUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION\nLesson 1: Introduction to Human-Computer \nInteraction\nJANUARY 2025\nMattheus Marcus Contreras \nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nLearning Objectives\nAt the end of this lecture, students should be able to:\n• Define Human–Computer Interaction (HCI)\n• Explain why HCI is important in modern technology\n• Identify the core components of HCI\n• Describe the principles of HCI\n• Recognize HCI’s impact on society and business\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nLesson Outline\nPart I. Human-Computer Interaction\nPart II. Core Components of Human-Computer Interaction\nPart III. Principles of Human-Computer Interaction\nPart IV. Fundamentals of Interaction Design\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nPart I\nHuman-Computer Interaction\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nWhat is Human–Computer Interaction?\no Human–Computer Interaction (HCI) is an interdisciplinary field.\no It focuses on the design, evaluation, and implementation of \ninteractive systems.\no It examines how people use technology and how technology \nresponds.\no The primary goal of HCI is to design systems that are useful, \nhumane, and desirable by effectively supporting user needs, \ncapabilities, and experiences.\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nFormal Definition of HCI\nHuman–Computer Interaction is “the study of people and computer \ntechnology and the ways these influence each other.” (Preece, \nRogers, & Sharp, 2015)\nHCI\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nWhy Study Human–Computer Interaction?\nTechnology is part of everyday life.\nPoor HCI Good HCI\nImproves user \nErrors satisfaction\nFrustration Reduces training time\nLow productivity Increases system \nadoption\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nHistory of Human–Computer Interaction\nEarly Computing Era (1940s–1960s)\no use of mainframe computers that occupied entire rooms\no Used by trained specialists only\no Interaction via punch cards\no Characteristics: Batch processing, no real-time interaction\nCommand Line Interfaces (CLI) Era (1960s–1980s)\no Text-based interaction\no Required memorization of commands\no Little to no visual feedback\no High cognitive load on users\no Characteristics: Expert-centered computing\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nHistory of Human–Computer Interaction\nFoundations of Interactive Systems (1960s–1970s)\no Introduction of keyboards and monitors\no Early interactive computing concepts\no Time-sharing systems\no Focus remained technical rather than user-focused\no Characteristics: Transition from batch to interactive systems\nPersonal Computing Revolution (late 1970s–1980s)\no Computers moved into homes and offices\no Users became non-experts\no Demand for easier and more intuitive interfaces\no Characteristics: Democratization of computing\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nHistory of Human–Computer Interaction\nWIMP Model / Graphical User Interfaces (1980s–1990s)\no Prevalence of Windows, Icons, Menus, and Pointers\no Became the standard desktop interaction model\no Xerox PARC, Apple, Microsoft\no Characteristics: Visual, direct manipulation interfaces\nInternet and Web Era (1990s–2000s)\no Web browsers as primary interfaces\no Hyperlinks and multimedia\no Growth of social computing\no Online collaboration and communication\no Characteristics: Information access and connectivity\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nHistory of Human–Computer Interaction\nMobile and Touch-Based Computing (mid-2000s–2010s)\no Smartphones and tablets\no Touch gestures\no Responsive web design\no Task-specific mobile applications\no Characteristic: Anytime, anywhere interaction\nModern Natural User Interfaces (2010s–present)\no Voice assistants\no Gesture recognition\no Mixed reality environments\no Reduced barriers to interaction\no Key characteristic: Natural, intuitive interaction styles\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nHistory of Human–Computer Interaction\nEmerging and Future Technologies (2020s–future)\no Artificial Intelligence (AI)\no Machine Learning\no Virtual and Augmented Reality (VR/AR)\no Internet of Things (IoT)\no Brain–computer interfaces\no Characteristics: Adaptive, intelligent, and immersive systems\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nPart II\nCore Components of Human-Computer \nInteraction\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nOverview of Core Components of HCI\nHCI consists of three interconnected components:\nThe Human (User)\nThe Computer (System)\nThe Interaction\nAll influenced by the context of use.\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nThe Human Component\no refers to the user/s of a computer system, including \nindividuals or groups, and encompasses their physical, \ncognitive, emotional, and social characteristics that influence \nhow they perceive, understand, and interact with technology. \no It considers factors such as age, experience, abilities, \nlimitations, cultural background, and personal preferences, all \nof which must be taken into account to design effective, \nusable, and user-centered systems.\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nThe Human Component\nHuman Capabilities and Limitations\no Physical abilities (vision, hearing, motor skills)\no Cognitive processes\n• Memory\n• Attention\n• Problem-solving\no Emotional factors\n• Motivation\n• Stress\n• Satisfaction\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nThe Computer Component\no refers to the technological systems that enable interaction, \nincluding both hardware and software elements that process \ninformation, support user input, and generate system output. \no It encompasses all computing technologies—such as desktops, \nmobile devices, embedded systems, wearable technologies, and \nemerging platforms like virtual reality and augmented reality—\nalong with their operating systems, applications, interfaces, \nand interaction mechanisms that facilitate communication \nbetween humans and digital systems.\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nThe Computer Component\nHardware\n• Input devices (keyboard, mouse, sensors)\n• Output devices (screens, speakers, haptics)\nSoftware\n• Operating systems\n• Applications\n• Interfaces\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nThe Interaction Component\no Refers to the communication between users and systems.\no Includes: (IPOF Model)\n• Input\n• Process\n• Output\n• Feedback\no Interaction is two-way and continuous\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nInput and Output Methods\nInput Methods\n• Keyboard and mouse (primary for PC/laptops)\n• Touchscreens (primary for mobile devices)\n• Voice input\n• Gesture control\n• Eye tracking\n• Sensors\nOutput Methods\n• Visual displays\n• Audio feedback\n• Haptic (vibration, force feedback)\n• Multimodal output (combination of methods)\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nPart III\nPrinciples of Human-Computer \nInteraction\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nPrinciples of Human–Computer Interaction\nUsability User-Centered Design\nUniversal Design Accessibility\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nUsability\nKey Attributes of Usability\no Learnability\no Efficiency\no Memorability\no Error prevention and recovery\no User satisfaction\nImportance of Usability\no System effectiveness\no User productivity\no User adoption and acceptance\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nUser-Centered Design (UCD)\nCharacteristics of User-Centered Design\no Focus on users throughout development\no Iterative design process\no Continuous user involvement\no Empirical evaluation\nComponents of User-Centered Design\no User research\no Task analysis\no Prototyping\no Usability testing\no Iterative refinement\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nUniversal Design\nCharacteristics of Universal Design\no Design for all users\no Inclusive from the start\no Flexible and adaptable solutions\nImportance of Universal Design\no Equitable use\no Flexibility in use\no Simple and intuitive operation\no Perceptible information\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nAccessibility\nCharacteristics of Accessibility \no Ensures equal access\no Supports inclusion\no Meets legal and ethical requirements\nTypes of Accessibility\no Visual accessibility\no Auditory accessibility\no Motor accessibility\no Cognitive accessibility\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nHolistic Interaction Design\no Combines usability, UCD, universal design, and accessibility\no Balances diverse user needs\no Maintains strong user focus\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nPart IV\nFundamentals of Interaction Design\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nWhat Are Design Principles?\nThese are general rules or guidelines that inform design \ndecisions.\nThey are derived from:\no Human psychology\no Ergonomics\no Past design successes and failures\no Applied across different systems and technologies\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nWhy Design Principles Matter\no Reduce user errors\no Improve learnability and efficiency\no Increase user satisfaction\no Ensure consistency and predictability\no Support inclusive and accessible design\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nPrinciples of Design in Human-Computer \nInteraction\no User Control\no Consistency\no Visibility\no Simplicity\no Affordance\no Feedback\no Error Prevention and Recovery\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nUser Control\no Users should feel in control of the system (User Freedom)\no Easy to exit, cancel, or reverse actions (Reversibility)\nExample: \n• Back Buttons\n• Create/Delete Features\n• Cancel Options\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nConsistency\no Similar actions produce similar results\no Interface elements behave the same way across the system\no Reduces learning effort and confusion\nExample: \nSame icon and shortcut for “Save” across applications\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nVisibility\no Important functions should be easily visible\no Users should not have to remember information unnecessarily\no Supports recognition rather than recall\nExample:\n• Clear and Visible Navigation Menus\n• Use of Top-Bottom, Left-Right Principle\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nSimplicity\no Eliminate unnecessary elements\no Present information clearly and concisely\no Avoid overwhelming users\nExample: \nMinimalist interfaces with clear priorities\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nAffordance\no Design should suggest how an element is used\no Users should understand actions without instructions\nExample: \n• Buttons that look clickable\n• Sliders that look draggable\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nFeedback\no System should always inform users about what is happening\no Feedback should be:\n• Immediate\n• Clear\n• Meaningful\nExample:\n• Displays/HUD/Dashboards\n• Loading Indicators\n• Immediate Confirmation Messages\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nError Prevention and Recovery\no Prevent errors before they occur\no Provide clear recovery options when errors happen\nExample:\n \n• Undo Functionality\n• Confirmation Dialogs\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nWhat is an Effective Design?\no Supports user goals\no Easy to learn and remember\no Efficient to use\no Accessible and inclusive\no Provides a positive user experience\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nCharacteristics of Effective Design\no Clear and intuitive layout \no Consistent visual and interaction patterns \no Responsive feedback\n \no Logical navigation\no Error-tolerant design\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nWhat is an Ineffective Design?\no Creates confusion or frustration\no Requires excessive learning or memorization\no Causes frequent user errors\no Lacks feedback or guidance\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nCharacteristics of Ineffective Design\no Inconsistent layouts and controls\no Hidden or unclear functions\no Poor feedback or no feedback\no Overloaded screens\no Difficult error recovery\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nEffective \nvs \nIneffective \nDesign\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nEffective \nvs \nIneffective \nDesign\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nEffective \nvs \nIneffective \nDesign\nCSIT238 - HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION COURSE ORIENTATION\nEND OF LESSON','Text extracted successfully.','READY','CSIT238 Lesson 1 - Introduction to HCI.pdf',46,'7eb9ac9500c7fa61362faf38237114e352088308aa4f9984a5aab5a6e1f47094',396169,'77550fb3-85d6-4ace-a4ee-15e44f5cd998.pdf','2026-11-12 11:39:02.332450',44);
